(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("terraform",{"terraform/iac-and-hcl-basics":{"html":"\u003cp>A teammate builds a network by clicking through a cloud console. Six months later nobody remembers which options they chose, the staging copy differs from production in ways no one can list, and rebuilding after a disaster is guesswork. Infrastructure as code (IaC) replaces memory and clicking with files that can be reviewed, repeated and tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What infrastructure as code gives you\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The Terraform workflow: init, plan, apply, destroy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HCL blocks, arguments and references\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to read a plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-iac\">Why IaC\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Repeatable\u003c/strong>: the same files create the same result in every environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Reviewable\u003c/strong>: changes appear as a diff that a colleague approves before anything happens\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Documented\u003c/strong>: the files are the truth about what exists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Recoverable\u003c/strong>: rebuilding is running a command, not recalling a procedure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Terraform is \u003cstrong>declarative\u003c/strong>. You describe the end result (&quot;a storage bucket with these settings&quot;) and Terraform works out the steps to get there from whatever exists now.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-workflow\">The workflow\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Command\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform init\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Downloads providers and modules, prepares the backend\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform fmt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Formats files consistently\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform validate\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Checks syntax and internal consistency\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shows what would change, without changing anything\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform apply\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Makes the changes (asks for confirmation)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform destroy\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Removes everything this configuration manages\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hcl-in-five-minutes\">HCL in five minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language) is built from \u003cstrong>blocks\u003c/strong> that contain \u003cstrong>arguments\u003c/strong>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform {\n  required_version = &quot;&gt;= 1.6&quot;\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = &quot;hashicorp/aws&quot;\n      version = &quot;~&gt; 5.0&quot;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nprovider &quot;aws&quot; {\n  region = &quot;eu-west-1&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;logs&quot; {\n  bucket = &quot;eknatha-demo-logs-12345&quot;\n\n  tags = {\n    Environment = &quot;dev&quot;\n    Owner       = &quot;platform&quot;\n  }\n}\n\noutput &quot;bucket_arn&quot; {\n  value = aws_s3_bucket.logs.arn\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">terraform\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  required_version\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&gt;= 1.6&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">required_providers\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    aws\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      source\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hashicorp/aws&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      version\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;~&gt; 5.0&quot;\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">provider\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  region\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;logs&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  bucket\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eknatha-demo-logs-12345&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">\n  tags\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    Environment\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dev&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    Owner\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">output\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;bucket_arn&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  value\u003c/span> = aws_s3_bucket.logs.arn\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read it this way:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>terraform\u003c/code> pins the Terraform and provider versions, so a teammate or a pipeline gets the same behaviour.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>provider\u003c/code> configures how to talk to a platform.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>resource &quot;TYPE&quot; &quot;NAME&quot;\u003c/code> declares one thing to manage. The pair of type and name (\u003ccode>aws_s3_bucket.logs\u003c/code>) is its address inside Terraform.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>aws_s3_bucket.logs.arn\u003c/code> is a \u003cstrong>reference\u003c/strong>. Terraform sees that the output depends on the bucket and orders the work correctly without being told.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>~&gt; 5.0\u003c/code> means &quot;5.x but not 6.0&quot;.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-plan\">Reading a plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform init\nterraform plan\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform init\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Terraform will perform the following actions:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_s3_bucket.logs will be created\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  + resource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;logs&quot; {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      + bucket = &quot;eknatha-demo-logs-12345&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      + id     = (known after apply)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      ...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is abbreviated sample output. The symbols matter:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Symbol\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>+\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Create\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>~\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Update in place\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>-\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Destroy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>-/+\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Destroy and recreate (replacement)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Always read the summary line and look for \u003ccode>-\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>-/+\u003c/code> before applying.\u003c/strong> A replacement of a database or a load balancer is the kind of change a plan exists to catch.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-tip\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Tip\u003c/div>\u003cp>Save a plan with \u003ccode>terraform plan -out=tfplan\u003c/code> and apply exactly that file with \u003ccode>terraform apply tfplan\u003c/code>. What was reviewed is then exactly what runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-plan-wants-to-destroy-the-database\">Incident: the plan wants to destroy the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small change to a tag is proposed in review. The plan output says \u003ccode>1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy\u003c/code>, and the destroyed resource is the production database.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_db_instance.main must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-/+ resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      ~ identifier = &quot;shop-prod&quot; -&gt; &quot;shop-production&quot; # forces replacement\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone renamed the \u003ccode>identifier\u003c/code> while &quot;tidying up&quot;. For that resource, a new identifier means a new database, so Terraform plans to delete the old one. The plan caught it because the reviewer read it. Revert the rename, rerun the plan, and confirm it now shows \u003ccode>No changes\u003c/code> or only the tag update. Protect the resource so this cannot happen quietly again:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  # ...\n  lifecycle {\n    prevent_destroy = true\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_db_instance&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># ...\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lifecycle\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    prevent_destroy\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>prevent_destroy\u003c/code>, any plan that would delete it fails with an error. It is a safety net, not a substitute for reading plans.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Applying without reading the plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing resources in the console, so reality drifts from the code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving provider versions unpinned\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the same names and state for every environment\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-iac","text":"Why IaC"},{"id":"the-workflow","text":"The workflow"},{"id":"hcl-in-five-minutes","text":"HCL in five minutes"},{"id":"reading-a-plan","text":"Reading a plan"},{"id":"incident-the-plan-wants-to-destroy-the-database","text":"Incident: the plan wants to destroy the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform documentation","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs"},{"t":"Terraform language","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language"},{"t":"Terraform CLI","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli"}],"text":"A teammate builds a network by clicking through a cloud console. Six months later nobody remembers which options they chose, the staging copy differs from production in ways no one can list, and rebuilding after a disaster is guesswork. Infrastructure as code (IaC) replaces memory and clicking with files that can be reviewed, repeated and tested. What you will learn What infrastructure as code gives you The Terraform workflow: init, plan, apply, destroy HCL blocks, arguments and references How to read a plan Why IaC Repeatable : the same files create the same result in every environment Reviewable : changes appear as a diff that a colleague approves before anything happens Documented : the files are the truth about what exists Recoverable : rebuilding is running a command, not recalling a procedure Terraform is declarative . You describe the end result (\"a storage bucket with these settings\") and Terraform works out the steps to get there from whatever exists now. The workflow Command What it does terraform init Downloads providers and modules, prepares the backend terraform fmt Formats files consistently terraform validate Checks syntax and internal consistency terraform plan Shows what would change, without changing anything terraform apply Makes the changes (asks for confirmation) terraform destroy Removes everything this configuration manages HCL in five minutes HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language) is built from blocks that contain arguments : terraform { required_version = \">= 1.6\" required_providers { aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\" version = \"~> 5.0\" } } } provider \"aws\" { region = \"eu-west-1\" } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"logs\" { bucket = \"eknatha-demo-logs-12345\" tags = { Environment = \"dev\" Owner = \"platform\" } } output \"bucket_arn\" { value = aws_s3_bucket.logs.arn } Read it this way: terraform pins the Terraform and provider versions, so a teammate or a pipeline gets the same behaviour. provider configures how to talk to a platform. resource \"TYPE\" \"NAME\" declares one thing to manage. The pair of type and name ( aws_s3_bucket.logs ) is its address inside Terraform. aws_s3_bucket.logs.arn is a reference . Terraform sees that the output depends on the bucket and orders the work correctly without being told. ~> 5.0 means \"5.x but not 6.0\". Reading a plan $ terraform init $ terraform plan Terraform will perform the following actions: # aws_s3_bucket.logs will be created + resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"logs\" { + bucket = \"eknatha-demo-logs-12345\" + id = (known after apply) ... } Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy. This is abbreviated sample output. The symbols matter: Symbol Meaning + Create ~ Update in place - Destroy -/+ Destroy and recreate (replacement) Always read the summary line and look for - and -/+ before applying. A replacement of a database or a load balancer is the kind of change a plan exists to catch. Tip Save a plan with terraform plan -out=tfplan and apply exactly that file with terraform apply tfplan . What was reviewed is then exactly what runs. Incident: the plan wants to destroy the database Incident A small change to a tag is proposed in review. The plan output says 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy , and the destroyed resource is the production database. # aws_db_instance.main must be replaced -/+ resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" { ~ identifier = \"shop-prod\" -> \"shop-production\" # forces replacement Someone renamed the identifier while \"tidying up\". For that resource, a new identifier means a new database, so Terraform plans to delete the old one. The plan caught it because the reviewer read it. Revert the rename, rerun the plan, and confirm it now shows No changes or only the tag update. Protect the resource so this cannot happen quietly again: resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" { # ... lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true } } With prevent_destroy , any plan that would delete it fails with an error. It is a safety net, not a substitute for reading plans. Common mistakes Applying without readin"},"terraform/providers-and-resources":{"html":"\u003cp>A bucket is a toy. Real infrastructure has a network, subnets, security groups and servers that refer to each other. Terraform handles this by building a \u003cstrong>dependency graph\u003c/strong> from your references, creating things in a valid order and in parallel where it can.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Providers and how they authenticate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resources with references and implicit dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data sources for things that already exist\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>count\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>depends_on\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>lifecycle\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"providers\">Providers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A provider is a plugin that knows how to manage one platform&#39;s API: AWS, Azure, Kubernetes, Cloudflare and many more. \u003ccode>terraform init\u003c/code> downloads the versions you pinned. Give providers credentials through the environment or the platform&#39;s own mechanisms (roles, workload identity), \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> through values written in your files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"export AWS_PROFILE=dev\nterraform init\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> AWS_PROFILE=dev\nterraform init\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-small-network-and-server\">A small network and server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  cidr_block           = &quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;\n  enable_dns_hostnames = true\n  tags                 = { Name = &quot;demo&quot; }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_subnet&quot; &quot;app&quot; {\n  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id\n  cidr_block        = &quot;10.20.1.0/24&quot;\n  availability_zone = &quot;eu-west-1a&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_security_group&quot; &quot;web&quot; {\n  name   = &quot;web&quot;\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = &quot;tcp&quot;\n    cidr_blocks = [&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;]\n  }\n\n  egress {\n    from_port   = 0\n    to_port     = 0\n    protocol    = &quot;-1&quot;\n    cidr_blocks = [&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;]\n  }\n}\n\ndata &quot;aws_ami&quot; &quot;al2023&quot; {\n  most_recent = true\n  owners      = [&quot;amazon&quot;]\n  filter {\n    name   = &quot;name&quot;\n    values = [&quot;al2023-ami-2023*-x86_64&quot;]\n  }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;web&quot; {\n  ami                    = data.aws_ami.al2023.id\n  instance_type          = &quot;t3.micro&quot;\n  subnet_id              = aws_subnet.app.id\n  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.web.id]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_vpc&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  cidr_block\u003c/span>           = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  enable_dns_hostnames\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  tags\u003c/span>                 = { Name = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;demo&quot;\u003c/span> }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_subnet&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span>            = aws_vpc.main.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  cidr_block\u003c/span>        = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.20.1.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  availability_zone\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1a&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_security_group&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span> = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  ingress {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    from_port\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    to_port\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    protocol\u003c/span>    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tcp&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    cidr_blocks\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  }\n\n  egress {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    from_port\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    to_port\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    protocol\u003c/span>    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-1&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    cidr_blocks\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">data\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_ami&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;al2023&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  most_recent\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  owners\u003c/span>      = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amazon&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  filter {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    name\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    values\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;al2023-ami-2023*-x86_64&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_instance&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  ami\u003c/span>                    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">data\u003c/span>.aws_ami.al2023.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  instance_type\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;t3.micro&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  subnet_id\u003c/span>              = aws_subnet.app.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_security_group_ids\u003c/span> = [aws_security_group.web.id]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Notice that \u003ccode>aws_subnet.app\u003c/code> uses \u003ccode>aws_vpc.main.id\u003c/code>. That reference is an \u003cstrong>implicit dependency\u003c/strong>: Terraform knows the VPC must exist first. A \u003cstrong>data source\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>data &quot;...&quot;\u003c/code>) reads something that already exists or is published, here the latest machine image, without managing it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"many-of-the-same-thing\">Many of the same thing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"variable &quot;subnets&quot; {\n  type = map(string)\n  default = {\n    a = &quot;10.20.1.0/24&quot;\n    b = &quot;10.20.2.0/24&quot;\n  }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_subnet&quot; &quot;multi&quot; {\n  for_each          = var.subnets\n  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id\n  cidr_block        = each.value\n  availability_zone = &quot;eu-west-1${each.key}&quot;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;subnets&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span> = map(string)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  default\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    a\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.20.1.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    b\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.20.2.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_subnet&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;multi&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  for_each\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.subnets\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span>            = aws_vpc.main.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  cidr_block\u003c/span>        = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">each\u003c/span>.value\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  availability_zone\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1${each.key}&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Meta-argument\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>count\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>N copies indexed by number (\u003ccode>[0]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code>). Removing an item from the middle shifts the rest\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One copy per key of a map or set. Each has a stable key such as \u003ccode>[&quot;a&quot;]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>depends_on\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An explicit dependency when Terraform cannot see one from references\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>lifecycle\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>prevent_destroy\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>create_before_destroy\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ignore_changes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Prefer \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code> over \u003ccode>count\u003c/code> for anything that is not a simple on/off switch (\u003ccode>count = var.enabled ? 1 : 0\u003c/code>). With \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>, deleting the second of three items makes Terraform rename the third, which can destroy and recreate a real resource.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspecting-the-graph-and-state\">Inspecting the graph and state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform state list\nterraform graph | head\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">aws_vpc.main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">aws_subnet.multi[&quot;a&quot;]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">aws_subnet.multi[&quot;b&quot;]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform graph | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-plan-wants-to-recreate-subnets-after-a-harmless-edit\">Incident: the plan wants to recreate subnets after a harmless edit\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>You delete the first of three subnets defined with \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>. The plan wants to destroy and recreate the other two.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_subnet.app[1] must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_subnet.app[2] must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 2 to add, 3 to destroy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>, items are tracked by position. Removing index 0 moves the old index 1 into position 0, so Terraform sees every address as changed. Switch the resource to \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code> over a map with stable keys, then tell Terraform that existing resources simply moved, using \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> blocks so nothing is destroyed:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"moved {\n  from = aws_subnet.app[1]\n  to   = aws_subnet.multi[&quot;b&quot;]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">moved\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  from\u003c/span> = aws_subnet.app[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  to\u003c/span>   = aws_subnet.multi[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;b&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rerun the plan and confirm it shows only the intended removal. Moves are refactors of the \u003cem>code\u003c/em>, not of the real infrastructure, and the \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> block expresses exactly that.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting credentials in configuration files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>count\u003c/code> for lists whose members may be removed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding AMI IDs that differ per region instead of using a data source\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Opening security groups to the whole internet on administrative ports\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},{"id":"a-small-network-and-server","text":"A small network and server"},{"id":"many-of-the-same-thing","text":"Many of the same thing"},{"id":"inspecting-the-graph-and-state","text":"Inspecting the graph and state"},{"id":"incident-the-plan-wants-to-recreate-subnets-after-a-harmless-edit","text":"Incident: the plan wants to recreate subnets after a harmless edit"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform: resources","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/resources"},{"t":"Terraform AWS provider","u":"https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs"},{"t":"Terraform AzureRM provider","u":"https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/azurerm/latest/docs"}],"text":"A bucket is a toy. Real infrastructure has a network, subnets, security groups and servers that refer to each other. Terraform handles this by building a dependency graph from your references, creating things in a valid order and in parallel where it can. What you will learn Providers and how they authenticate Resources with references and implicit dependencies Data sources for things that already exist count , for_each , depends_on and lifecycle Providers A provider is a plugin that knows how to manage one platform&#39;s API: AWS, Azure, Kubernetes, Cloudflare and many more. terraform init downloads the versions you pinned. Give providers credentials through the environment or the platform&#39;s own mechanisms (roles, workload identity), not through values written in your files. export AWS_PROFILE=dev terraform init A small network and server resource \"aws_vpc\" \"main\" { cidr_block = \"10.20.0.0/16\" enable_dns_hostnames = true tags = { Name = \"demo\" } } resource \"aws_subnet\" \"app\" { vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id cidr_block = \"10.20.1.0/24\" availability_zone = \"eu-west-1a\" } resource \"aws_security_group\" \"web\" { name = \"web\" vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id ingress { from_port = 443 to_port = 443 protocol = \"tcp\" cidr_blocks = [ \"0.0.0.0/0\" ] } egress { from_port = 0 to_port = 0 protocol = \"-1\" cidr_blocks = [ \"0.0.0.0/0\" ] } } data \"aws_ami\" \"al2023\" { most_recent = true owners = [ \"amazon\" ] filter { name = \"name\" values = [ \"al2023-ami-2023*-x86_64\" ] } } resource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" { ami = data .aws_ami.al2023.id instance_type = \"t3.micro\" subnet_id = aws_subnet.app.id vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.web.id] } Notice that aws_subnet.app uses aws_vpc.main.id . That reference is an implicit dependency : Terraform knows the VPC must exist first. A data source ( data \"...\" ) reads something that already exists or is published, here the latest machine image, without managing it. Many of the same thing variable \"subnets\" { type = map(string) default = { a = \"10.20.1.0/24\" b = \"10.20.2.0/24\" } } resource \"aws_subnet\" \"multi\" { for_each = var .subnets vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id cidr_block = each .value availability_zone = \"eu-west-1${each.key}\" } Meta-argument Use count N copies indexed by number ( [0] , [1] ). Removing an item from the middle shifts the rest for_each One copy per key of a map or set. Each has a stable key such as [\"a\"] depends_on An explicit dependency when Terraform cannot see one from references lifecycle prevent_destroy , create_before_destroy , ignore_changes Prefer for_each over count for anything that is not a simple on/off switch ( count = var.enabled ? 1 : 0 ). With count , deleting the second of three items makes Terraform rename the third, which can destroy and recreate a real resource. Inspecting the graph and state $ terraform state list aws_vpc.main aws_subnet.multi[\"a\"] aws_subnet.multi[\"b\"] $ terraform graph | head Incident: the plan wants to recreate subnets after a harmless edit Incident You delete the first of three subnets defined with count . The plan wants to destroy and recreate the other two. # aws_subnet.app[1] must be replaced # aws_subnet.app[2] must be replaced Plan: 2 to add, 3 to destroy With count , items are tracked by position. Removing index 0 moves the old index 1 into position 0, so Terraform sees every address as changed. Switch the resource to for_each over a map with stable keys, then tell Terraform that existing resources simply moved, using moved blocks so nothing is destroyed: moved { from = aws_subnet.app[ 1 ] to = aws_subnet.multi[ \"b\" ] } Rerun the plan and confirm it shows only the intended removal. Moves are refactors of the code , not of the real infrastructure, and the moved block expresses exactly that. Common mistakes Putting credentials in configuration files Using count for lists whose members may be removed Hard-coding AMI IDs that differ per region instead of using a data source Opening security groups to the whole internet on administrative ports"},"terraform/variables-and-outputs":{"html":"\u003cp>Hard-coded values make a configuration usable exactly once. Variables turn it into something you can reuse for dev, staging and production, and validation catches bad input before a cloud API call does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Input variables with types, defaults and validation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ways to supply values and their order of precedence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locals for computed values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Outputs, and handling sensitive values\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"input-variables\">Input variables\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"variable &quot;environment&quot; {\n  type        = string\n  description = &quot;Deployment environment&quot;\n\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([&quot;dev&quot;, &quot;staging&quot;, &quot;prod&quot;], var.environment)\n    error_message = &quot;environment must be dev, staging or prod.&quot;\n  }\n}\n\nvariable &quot;instance_count&quot; {\n  type    = number\n  default = 2\n}\n\nvariable &quot;allowed_cidrs&quot; {\n  type    = list(string)\n  default = [&quot;10.0.0.0/8&quot;]\n}\n\nvariable &quot;db_password&quot; {\n  type      = string\n  sensitive = true\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;environment&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>        = string\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  description\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Deployment environment&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n  validation {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    condition\u003c/span>     = contains([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dev&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;staging&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.environment)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    error_message\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;environment must be dev, staging or prod.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;instance_count&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>    = number\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  default\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;allowed_cidrs&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>    = list(string)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  default\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.0.0.0/8&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db_password&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>      = string\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  sensitive\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always give variables a \u003ccode>type\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>description\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>sensitive = true\u003c/code> hides the value in plan and apply output (but not from the state file, which the next lesson covers).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"supplying-values\">Supplying values\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Terraform picks a value using this order, with later sources winning:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>The variable&#39;s \u003ccode>default\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environment variables named \u003ccode>TF_VAR_name\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>terraform.tfvars\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>*.auto.tfvars\u003c/code> files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>-var-file=\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>-var\u003c/code> options on the command line\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# prod.tfvars\nenvironment    = &quot;prod&quot;\ninstance_count = 4\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># prod.tfvars\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment\u003c/span>    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">instance_count\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan -var-file=prod.tfvars\nexport TF_VAR_db_password=&quot;$(cat /run/secrets/db_password)&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan -var-file=prod.tfvars\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> TF_VAR_db_password=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(cat /run/secrets/db_password)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Never write real secrets into \u003ccode>.tfvars\u003c/code> files that are shared. Pass them from the environment or from a secret manager at run time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locals\">Locals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Locals give names to expressions so you write them once:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"locals {\n  name_prefix = &quot;shop-${var.environment}&quot;\n\n  common_tags = {\n    Environment = var.environment\n    Owner       = &quot;platform&quot;\n    ManagedBy   = &quot;terraform&quot;\n  }\n\n  is_prod = var.environment == &quot;prod&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;assets&quot; {\n  bucket = &quot;${local.name_prefix}-assets&quot;\n  tags   = local.common_tags\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">locals\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name_prefix\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-${var.environment}&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">\n  common_tags\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    Environment\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.environment\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    Owner\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    ManagedBy\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;terraform&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">\n  is_prod\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.environment == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;assets&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  bucket\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;${local.name_prefix}-assets&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  tags\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span>.common_tags\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use locals for derived values and variables for inputs. Do not overuse locals to the point where readers must jump around to follow the code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expressions-you-will-use-constantly\">Expressions you will use constantly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Expression\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Result\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Conditional\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>local.is_prod ? &quot;m5.large&quot; : &quot;t3.micro&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Chooses a value\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>for\u003c/code> expression\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>[for s in var.names : upper(s)]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Transforms a list\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>String template\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>&quot;${var.environment}-api&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Builds a string\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Functions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>merge(a, b)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>lookup(map, key, default)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cidrsubnet(cidr, 8, 1)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Built-in helpers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>terraform console\u003c/code> to try expressions without applying anything:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform console\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform console\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt; cidrsubnet(&quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;, 8, 3)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;10.20.3.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt; [for s in [&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;] : upper(s)]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  &quot;A&quot;,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  &quot;B&quot;,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"outputs\">Outputs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"output &quot;assets_bucket&quot; {\n  description = &quot;Name of the assets bucket&quot;\n  value       = aws_s3_bucket.assets.bucket\n}\n\noutput &quot;db_endpoint&quot; {\n  value     = aws_db_instance.main.endpoint\n  sensitive = true\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">output\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;assets_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  description\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Name of the assets bucket&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  value\u003c/span>       = aws_s3_bucket.assets.bucket\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">output\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db_endpoint&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  value\u003c/span>     = aws_db_instance.main.endpoint\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  sensitive\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Outputs print after \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> and are how modules return values to their callers. Read one with \u003ccode>terraform output assets_bucket\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-secret-shows-up-in-a-pipeline-log\">Incident: a secret shows up in a pipeline log\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A reviewer notices a database password in a CI job&#39;s plan output.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The variable had no \u003ccode>sensitive = true\u003c/code>, so Terraform printed it in the plan. First \u003cstrong>rotate the password\u003c/strong>, because anything printed to a log must be treated as exposed. Then fix the cause:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"variable &quot;db_password&quot; {\n  type      = string\n  sensitive = true\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db_password&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>      = string\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  sensitive\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Also mark any output that exposes a derived secret as \u003ccode>sensitive\u003c/code>, and make sure the value arrives through \u003ccode>TF_VAR_db_password\u003c/code> from your CI system&#39;s secret store instead of a file. Finally, remember \u003ccode>sensitive\u003c/code> only masks display. The value still lives in the state file, so state must be stored in an encrypted backend with restricted access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Variables without types, so a string sneaks in where a number is expected\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets in \u003ccode>.tfvars\u003c/code> files that are shared\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that \u003ccode>sensitive\u003c/code> does not protect state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Constructing complex logic in locals that nobody can read\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"input-variables","text":"Input variables"},{"id":"supplying-values","text":"Supplying values"},{"id":"locals","text":"Locals"},{"id":"expressions-you-will-use-constantly","text":"Expressions you will use constantly"},{"id":"outputs","text":"Outputs"},{"id":"incident-a-secret-shows-up-in-a-pipeline-log","text":"Incident: a secret shows up in a pipeline log"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform: input variables","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/variables"},{"t":"Terraform language","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language"},{"t":"Terraform: built-in functions","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/functions"}],"text":"Hard-coded values make a configuration usable exactly once. Variables turn it into something you can reuse for dev, staging and production, and validation catches bad input before a cloud API call does. What you will learn Input variables with types, defaults and validation Ways to supply values and their order of precedence Locals for computed values Outputs, and handling sensitive values Input variables variable \"environment\" { type = string description = \"Deployment environment\" validation { condition = contains([ \"dev\" , \"staging\" , \"prod\" ], var .environment) error_message = \"environment must be dev, staging or prod.\" } } variable \"instance_count\" { type = number default = 2 } variable \"allowed_cidrs\" { type = list(string) default = [ \"10.0.0.0/8\" ] } variable \"db_password\" { type = string sensitive = true } Always give variables a type and a description . sensitive = true hides the value in plan and apply output (but not from the state file, which the next lesson covers). Supplying values Terraform picks a value using this order, with later sources winning: The variable&#39;s default Environment variables named TF_VAR_name terraform.tfvars and *.auto.tfvars files -var-file= and -var options on the command line # prod.tfvars environment = \"prod\" instance_count = 4 $ terraform plan -var-file=prod.tfvars $ export TF_VAR_db_password= \" $(cat /run/secrets/db_password) \" Never write real secrets into .tfvars files that are shared. Pass them from the environment or from a secret manager at run time. Locals Locals give names to expressions so you write them once: locals { name_prefix = \"shop-${var.environment}\" common_tags = { Environment = var .environment Owner = \"platform\" ManagedBy = \"terraform\" } is_prod = var .environment == \"prod\" } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"assets\" { bucket = \"${local.name_prefix}-assets\" tags = local .common_tags } Use locals for derived values and variables for inputs. Do not overuse locals to the point where readers must jump around to follow the code. Expressions you will use constantly Expression Example Result Conditional local.is_prod ? \"m5.large\" : \"t3.micro\" Chooses a value for expression [for s in var.names : upper(s)] Transforms a list String template \"${var.environment}-api\" Builds a string Functions merge(a, b) , lookup(map, key, default) , cidrsubnet(cidr, 8, 1) Built-in helpers Use terraform console to try expressions without applying anything: $ terraform console > cidrsubnet(\"10.20.0.0/16\", 8, 3) \"10.20.3.0/24\" > [for s in [\"a\",\"b\"] : upper(s)] [ \"A\", \"B\", ] Outputs output \"assets_bucket\" { description = \"Name of the assets bucket\" value = aws_s3_bucket.assets.bucket } output \"db_endpoint\" { value = aws_db_instance.main.endpoint sensitive = true } Outputs print after apply and are how modules return values to their callers. Read one with terraform output assets_bucket . Incident: a secret shows up in a pipeline log Incident A reviewer notices a database password in a CI job&#39;s plan output. The variable had no sensitive = true , so Terraform printed it in the plan. First rotate the password , because anything printed to a log must be treated as exposed. Then fix the cause: variable \"db_password\" { type = string sensitive = true } Also mark any output that exposes a derived secret as sensitive , and make sure the value arrives through TF_VAR_db_password from your CI system&#39;s secret store instead of a file. Finally, remember sensitive only masks display. The value still lives in the state file, so state must be stored in an encrypted backend with restricted access. Common mistakes Variables without types, so a string sneaks in where a number is expected Secrets in .tfvars files that are shared Forgetting that sensitive does not protect state Constructing complex logic in locals that nobody can read"},"terraform/expressions-and-dynamic-blocks":{"html":"\u003cp>Real infrastructure is repetitive: ten subnets, five security-group rules, three environments. Copying resource blocks is error-prone. Terraform&#39;s expression language lets you describe the pattern once and drive it from data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>count\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code>, and why the difference matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>for\u003c/code> expressions, conditionals and common functions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>dynamic\u003c/code> blocks for repeated nested configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping configurations readable\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"count-and-for-each\">count and for_each\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>count\u003c/code> creates N copies addressed by index. \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code> creates one instance per key in a map or set, addressed by that key.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"variable &quot;subnets&quot; {\n  type = map(object({\n    cidr = string\n    az   = string\n  }))\n  default = {\n    app-a = { cidr = &quot;10.0.1.0/24&quot;, az = &quot;eu-west-1a&quot; }\n    app-b = { cidr = &quot;10.0.2.0/24&quot;, az = &quot;eu-west-1b&quot; }\n    db-a  = { cidr = &quot;10.0.11.0/24&quot;, az = &quot;eu-west-1a&quot; }\n  }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_subnet&quot; &quot;this&quot; {\n  for_each          = var.subnets\n  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id\n  cidr_block        = each.value.cidr\n  availability_zone = each.value.az\n  tags = {\n    Name = &quot;shop-${each.key}&quot;\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;subnets&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span> = map(object({\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    cidr\u003c/span> = string\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    az\u003c/span>   = string\n  }))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  default\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    app-a\u003c/span> = { cidr = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.0.1.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>, az = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1a&quot;\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    app-b\u003c/span> = { cidr = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.0.2.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>, az = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1b&quot;\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    db-a\u003c/span>  = { cidr = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.0.11.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>, az = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1a&quot;\u003c/span> }\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_subnet&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;this&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  for_each\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.subnets\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span>            = aws_vpc.main.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  cidr_block\u003c/span>        = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">each\u003c/span>.value.cidr\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  availability_zone\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">each\u003c/span>.value.az\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  tags\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    Name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-${each.key}&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each subnet is tracked as \u003ccode>aws_subnet.this[&quot;app-a&quot;]\u003c/code>. If you later remove \u003ccode>app-b\u003c/code> from the map, Terraform destroys only that subnet. With \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>, instances are tracked by position, so removing the \u003cstrong>middle\u003c/strong> item shifts every later index and Terraform plans to destroy and recreate resources that did not change. Prefer \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code> whenever the items have a natural name. Use \u003ccode>count\u003c/code> mainly for a simple on/off switch: \u003ccode>count = var.enabled ? 1 : 0\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"for-expressions-and-functions\">for expressions and functions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> expression transforms a list or map into another.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"locals {\n  names      = [for k, v in var.subnets : &quot;${k}-${v.az}&quot;]\n  app_cidrs  = [for k, v in var.subnets : v.cidr if startswith(k, &quot;app&quot;)]\n  az_by_name = { for k, v in var.subnets : k =&gt; v.az }\n\n  common_tags = {\n    Project = &quot;shop&quot;\n    Env     = terraform.workspace\n  }\n  all_tags = merge(local.common_tags, { Owner = &quot;platform&quot; })\n}\n\noutput &quot;app_cidrs&quot; {\n  value = local.app_cidrs\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">locals\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  names\u003c/span>      = [for k, v in \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.subnets : \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;${k}-${v.az}&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  app_cidrs\u003c/span>  = [for k, v in \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.subnets : v.cidr if startswith(k, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>)]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  az_by_name\u003c/span> = { for k, v in \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.subnets : k =&gt; v.az }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">\n  common_tags\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    Project\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    Env\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">terraform\u003c/span>.workspace\n  }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  all_tags\u003c/span> = merge(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span>.common_tags, { Owner = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform&quot;\u003c/span> })\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">output\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app_cidrs&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  value\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span>.app_cidrs\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The functions you will use most: \u003ccode>merge\u003c/code> for maps, \u003ccode>concat\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>flatten\u003c/code> for lists, \u003ccode>lookup\u003c/code> with a default, \u003ccode>coalesce\u003c/code> for the first non-empty value, \u003ccode>cidrsubnet\u003c/code> for carving address ranges, \u003ccode>format\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>join\u003c/code> for strings, and \u003ccode>try\u003c/code> to fall back when an expression fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"locals {\n  vpc_cidr = &quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;\n  subnet_cidrs = [for i in range(3) : cidrsubnet(local.vpc_cidr, 8, i)]\n  instance_type = lookup({ dev = &quot;t3.small&quot;, prod = &quot;m6i.large&quot; }, terraform.workspace, &quot;t3.small&quot;)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">locals\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_cidr\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  subnet_cidrs\u003c/span> = [for i in range(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>) : cidrsubnet(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span>.vpc_cidr, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, i)]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  instance_type\u003c/span> = lookup({ dev = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;t3.small&quot;\u003c/span>, prod = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;m6i.large&quot;\u003c/span> }, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">terraform\u003c/span>.workspace, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;t3.small&quot;\u003c/span>)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Test expressions interactively with \u003ccode>terraform console\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform console\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform console\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt; cidrsubnet(&quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;, 8, 2)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;10.20.2.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt; [for s in [&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;] : upper(s)]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dynamic-blocks\">dynamic blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some resources contain repeated nested blocks, such as ingress rules in a security group. A \u003ccode>dynamic\u003c/code> block generates one nested block per element.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"variable &quot;ingress_rules&quot; {\n  type = list(object({\n    port = number\n    cidr = string\n  }))\n  default = [\n    { port = 443, cidr = &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot; },\n    { port = 22, cidr = &quot;10.0.0.0/8&quot; },\n  ]\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_security_group&quot; &quot;web&quot; {\n  name   = &quot;shop-web&quot;\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  dynamic &quot;ingress&quot; {\n    for_each = var.ingress_rules\n    content {\n      from_port   = ingress.value.port\n      to_port     = ingress.value.port\n      protocol    = &quot;tcp&quot;\n      cidr_blocks = [ingress.value.cidr]\n    }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ingress_rules&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span> = list(object({\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    port\u003c/span> = number\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    cidr\u003c/span> = string\n  }))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  default\u003c/span> = [\n    { port = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span>, cidr = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\u003c/span> },\n    { port = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">22\u003c/span>, cidr = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.0.0.0/8&quot;\u003c/span> },\n  ]\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_security_group&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-web&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span> = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">dynamic\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ingress&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    for_each\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.ingress_rules\n    content {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      from_port\u003c/span>   = ingress.value.port\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      to_port\u003c/span>     = ingress.value.port\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      protocol\u003c/span>    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tcp&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      cidr_blocks\u003c/span> = [ingress.value.cidr]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Inside \u003ccode>content\u003c/code>, the iterator is named after the block (\u003ccode>ingress\u003c/code>) and exposes \u003ccode>.key\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>.value\u003c/code>. Use dynamic blocks only when the data really varies. Overuse makes configuration hard to read, so a plain repeated block is better for two fixed rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conditionals-and-validation\">Conditionals and validation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"variable &quot;environment&quot; {\n  type = string\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([&quot;dev&quot;, &quot;staging&quot;, &quot;prod&quot;], var.environment)\n    error_message = &quot;environment must be dev, staging or prod.&quot;\n  }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  identifier          = &quot;shop-${var.environment}&quot;\n  instance_class      = var.environment == &quot;prod&quot; ? &quot;db.m6i.large&quot; : &quot;db.t3.medium&quot;\n  multi_az            = var.environment == &quot;prod&quot;\n  deletion_protection = var.environment == &quot;prod&quot;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;environment&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span> = string\n  validation {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    condition\u003c/span>     = contains([\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dev&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;staging&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.environment)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    error_message\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;environment must be dev, staging or prod.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_db_instance&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  identifier\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-${var.environment}&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  instance_class\u003c/span>      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.environment == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span> ? \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db.m6i.large&quot;\u003c/span> : \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db.t3.medium&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  multi_az\u003c/span>            = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.environment == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  deletion_protection\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.environment == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-plan-that-wants-to-destroy-the-whole-fleet\">Incident: the plan that wants to destroy the whole fleet\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Engineers remove one server name from the list that drives \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>. The next \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> shows 14 resources to destroy and 14 to create, including production instances that did not change.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan | grep -E &quot;must be replaced|will be destroyed&quot; | head\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;must be replaced|will be destroyed&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_instance.web[1] must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_instance.web[2] must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The list shifted, so every instance after the removed one received a new index and a new identity. Convert to \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code> and tell Terraform the instances are the same, using \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> blocks:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"moved {\n  from = aws_instance.web[1]\n  to   = aws_instance.web[&quot;web-b&quot;]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">moved\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  from\u003c/span> = aws_instance.web[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  to\u003c/span>   = aws_instance.web[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-b&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> again and confirm it shows no destroys before applying. Make it policy that anything keyed by names uses \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>count\u003c/code> over a list whose items can be removed from the middle\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>for_each\u003c/code> with values not known until apply\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overusing \u003ccode>dynamic\u003c/code> blocks so readers cannot see the resulting resource\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Complex nested expressions in resource arguments instead of a named local\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing with \u003ccode>terraform console\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> first\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"count-and-for-each","text":"count and for_each"},{"id":"for-expressions-and-functions","text":"for expressions and functions"},{"id":"dynamic-blocks","text":"dynamic blocks"},{"id":"conditionals-and-validation","text":"Conditionals and validation"},{"id":"incident-the-plan-that-wants-to-destroy-the-whole-fleet","text":"Incident: the plan that wants to destroy the whole fleet"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform: expressions","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/expressions"},{"t":"Terraform: for_each","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/meta-arguments/for_each"},{"t":"Terraform: dynamic blocks","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/expressions/dynamic-blocks"}],"text":"Real infrastructure is repetitive: ten subnets, five security-group rules, three environments. Copying resource blocks is error-prone. Terraform&#39;s expression language lets you describe the pattern once and drive it from data. What you will learn count versus for_each , and why the difference matters for expressions, conditionals and common functions dynamic blocks for repeated nested configuration Keeping configurations readable count and for_each count creates N copies addressed by index. for_each creates one instance per key in a map or set, addressed by that key. variable \"subnets\" { type = map(object({ cidr = string az = string })) default = { app-a = { cidr = \"10.0.1.0/24\" , az = \"eu-west-1a\" } app-b = { cidr = \"10.0.2.0/24\" , az = \"eu-west-1b\" } db-a = { cidr = \"10.0.11.0/24\" , az = \"eu-west-1a\" } } } resource \"aws_subnet\" \"this\" { for_each = var .subnets vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id cidr_block = each .value.cidr availability_zone = each .value.az tags = { Name = \"shop-${each.key}\" } } Each subnet is tracked as aws_subnet.this[\"app-a\"] . If you later remove app-b from the map, Terraform destroys only that subnet. With count , instances are tracked by position, so removing the middle item shifts every later index and Terraform plans to destroy and recreate resources that did not change. Prefer for_each whenever the items have a natural name. Use count mainly for a simple on/off switch: count = var.enabled ? 1 : 0 . for expressions and functions A for expression transforms a list or map into another. locals { names = [for k, v in var .subnets : \"${k}-${v.az}\" ] app_cidrs = [for k, v in var .subnets : v.cidr if startswith(k, \"app\" )] az_by_name = { for k, v in var .subnets : k => v.az } common_tags = { Project = \"shop\" Env = terraform .workspace } all_tags = merge( local .common_tags, { Owner = \"platform\" }) } output \"app_cidrs\" { value = local .app_cidrs } The functions you will use most: merge for maps, concat and flatten for lists, lookup with a default, coalesce for the first non-empty value, cidrsubnet for carving address ranges, format and join for strings, and try to fall back when an expression fails. locals { vpc_cidr = \"10.20.0.0/16\" subnet_cidrs = [for i in range( 3 ) : cidrsubnet( local .vpc_cidr, 8 , i)] instance_type = lookup({ dev = \"t3.small\" , prod = \"m6i.large\" }, terraform .workspace, \"t3.small\" ) } Test expressions interactively with terraform console : $ terraform console > cidrsubnet(\"10.20.0.0/16\", 8, 2) \"10.20.2.0/24\" > [for s in [\"a\",\"b\"] : upper(s)] dynamic blocks Some resources contain repeated nested blocks, such as ingress rules in a security group. A dynamic block generates one nested block per element. variable \"ingress_rules\" { type = list(object({ port = number cidr = string })) default = [ { port = 443 , cidr = \"0.0.0.0/0\" }, { port = 22 , cidr = \"10.0.0.0/8\" }, ] } resource \"aws_security_group\" \"web\" { name = \"shop-web\" vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id dynamic \"ingress\" { for_each = var .ingress_rules content { from_port = ingress.value.port to_port = ingress.value.port protocol = \"tcp\" cidr_blocks = [ingress.value.cidr] } } } Inside content , the iterator is named after the block ( ingress ) and exposes .key and .value . Use dynamic blocks only when the data really varies. Overuse makes configuration hard to read, so a plain repeated block is better for two fixed rules. Conditionals and validation variable \"environment\" { type = string validation { condition = contains([ \"dev\" , \"staging\" , \"prod\" ], var .environment) error_message = \"environment must be dev, staging or prod.\" } } resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" { identifier = \"shop-${var.environment}\" instance_class = var .environment == \"prod\" ? \"db.m6i.large\" : \"db.t3.medium\" multi_az = var .environment == \"prod\" deletion_protection = var .environment == \"prod\" } Incident: the plan that wants to destroy the whole fleet Incident Engineers remove one server name from the list that drives count . The next terraform plan shows 14 resources to de"},"terraform/state":{"html":"\u003cp>Terraform has to remember which real resources belong to which blocks in your code. That memory is the \u003cstrong>state\u003c/strong>. It is the most important and most dangerous file in the system: lose it and Terraform forgets what it created, corrupt it and plans become nonsense, expose it and you leak secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What state contains and why it matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remote backends and state locking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Importing existing resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe use of the \u003ccode>terraform state\u003c/code> commands\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-state-is\">What state is\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>State maps each resource address (\u003ccode>aws_s3_bucket.logs\u003c/code>) to the real object&#39;s ID and records its attributes. During \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code>, Terraform refreshes state from the real world, compares it with your code, and computes the difference.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>State also contains \u003cstrong>every attribute\u003c/strong>, including passwords and keys that you marked sensitive. Treat it as a secret.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Never edit the state file by hand and never share it over chat or email. Keep it out of any repository, and restrict who can read the storage that holds it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"remote-backends-and-locking\">Remote backends and locking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A local \u003ccode>terraform.tfstate\u003c/code> file works for experiments. On a team it fails quickly: two people run \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> at once, or one person&#39;s copy is out of date. A \u003cstrong>remote backend\u003c/strong> stores state centrally, and \u003cstrong>locking\u003c/strong> stops two runs from changing it simultaneously.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform {\n  backend &quot;s3&quot; {\n    bucket       = &quot;acme-terraform-state&quot;\n    key          = &quot;shop/prod/terraform.tfstate&quot;\n    region       = &quot;eu-west-1&quot;\n    encrypt      = true\n    use_lockfile = true\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">terraform\u003c/span> {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">backend\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    bucket\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-terraform-state&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    key\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop/prod/terraform.tfstate&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    region\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    encrypt\u003c/span>      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    use_lockfile\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This stores state in an S3 bucket with encryption and uses S3&#39;s own lock file support (recent Terraform releases). Older setups use a DynamoDB table for locks, and you will still see \u003ccode>dynamodb_table\u003c/code> in existing code. For the storage itself, enable bucket \u003cstrong>versioning\u003c/strong> so you can recover a previous state, block public access, and allow only the pipeline and a small group to read it. Azure Storage and Terraform Cloud-style services provide the same ideas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Use a separate \u003ccode>key\u003c/code> (or a separate bucket or account) per environment, so a mistake in dev can never touch production state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"importing-existing-resources\">Importing existing resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When something already exists in the cloud, bring it under management instead of recreating it. With current Terraform, write the target in code and add an \u003ccode>import\u003c/code> block:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import {\n  to = aws_s3_bucket.legacy\n  id = &quot;acme-legacy-reports&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;legacy&quot; {\n  bucket = &quot;acme-legacy-reports&quot;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  to\u003c/span> = aws_s3_bucket.legacy\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  id\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-legacy-reports&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;legacy&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  bucket\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-legacy-reports&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code>, which shows the import, and \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> to record it. Then keep running \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> and adjust the code until it reports no changes. The older command form is \u003ccode>terraform import aws_s3_bucket.legacy acme-legacy-reports\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"state-commands\">State commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform state list\nterraform state show aws_s3_bucket.logs\nterraform state mv aws_s3_bucket.logs aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs\nterraform state rm aws_s3_bucket.legacy\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state show aws_s3_bucket.logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mv\u003c/span> aws_s3_bucket.logs aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> aws_s3_bucket.legacy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Command\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>state list\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>show\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Read-only inspection\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>state mv\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Renames the address in state only (prefer a \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> block, which is reviewable)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>state rm\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Makes Terraform forget a resource \u003cstrong>without\u003c/strong> deleting it in the cloud\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>force-unlock ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Releases a stuck lock. Use only if you are certain no run is active\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-error-acquiring-the-state-lock\">Incident: &quot;Error acquiring the state lock&quot;\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline run fails immediately with \u003ccode>Error: Error acquiring the state lock\u003c/code>, and the message shows a lock held by another run.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: Error acquiring the state lock\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Lock Info:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  ID:        3f6c0a1e-...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Operation: OperationTypeApply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Who:       ci-runner@build-17\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Created:   2026-10-24 02:14:09 UTC\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>First find out whether that run is genuinely still going. Look at your CI system for job \u003ccode>build-17\u003c/code>. If it is still applying, \u003cstrong>wait\u003c/strong>: unlocking during an apply is how state gets corrupted. If the job crashed or was cancelled and clearly is not running, release the lock deliberately:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform force-unlock 3f6c0a1e-...\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform force-unlock 3f6c0a1e-...\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then run \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> to check that state and reality agree. Prevent recurrences by allowing one run per environment at a time in the pipeline, and by never killing an apply mid-way.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-plan\">Predict the plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Someone deletes a resource by hand in the console. You run terraform plan. What does it show?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It shows the resource will be created again.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Plan compares code and state with reality after a refresh. Missing in reality means create.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two engineers run apply at the same time against a remote state without locking. What can happen?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> State can be overwritten or corrupted, losing track of resources.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a backend with locking so a second apply waits or fails.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keeping state in a repository or on a laptop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No encryption, versioning or access control on the state bucket\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One giant state for everything, so every plan is slow and a mistake can touch anything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running \u003ccode>force-unlock\u003c/code> without checking for an active run\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-state-is","text":"What state is"},{"id":"remote-backends-and-locking","text":"Remote backends and locking"},{"id":"importing-existing-resources","text":"Importing existing resources"},{"id":"state-commands","text":"State commands"},{"id":"incident-error-acquiring-the-state-lock","text":"Incident: Error acquiring the state lock"},{"id":"predict-the-plan","text":"Predict the plan"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform: state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state"},{"t":"Terraform: backends","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/backend"},{"t":"Terraform CLI","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli"}],"text":"Terraform has to remember which real resources belong to which blocks in your code. That memory is the state . It is the most important and most dangerous file in the system: lose it and Terraform forgets what it created, corrupt it and plans become nonsense, expose it and you leak secrets. What you will learn What state contains and why it matters Remote backends and state locking Importing existing resources Safe use of the terraform state commands What state is State maps each resource address ( aws_s3_bucket.logs ) to the real object&#39;s ID and records its attributes. During plan , Terraform refreshes state from the real world, compares it with your code, and computes the difference. State also contains every attribute , including passwords and keys that you marked sensitive. Treat it as a secret. Warning Never edit the state file by hand and never share it over chat or email. Keep it out of any repository, and restrict who can read the storage that holds it. Remote backends and locking A local terraform.tfstate file works for experiments. On a team it fails quickly: two people run apply at once, or one person&#39;s copy is out of date. A remote backend stores state centrally, and locking stops two runs from changing it simultaneously. terraform { backend \"s3\" { bucket = \"acme-terraform-state\" key = \"shop/prod/terraform.tfstate\" region = \"eu-west-1\" encrypt = true use_lockfile = true } } This stores state in an S3 bucket with encryption and uses S3&#39;s own lock file support (recent Terraform releases). Older setups use a DynamoDB table for locks, and you will still see dynamodb_table in existing code. For the storage itself, enable bucket versioning so you can recover a previous state, block public access, and allow only the pipeline and a small group to read it. Azure Storage and Terraform Cloud-style services provide the same ideas. Use a separate key (or a separate bucket or account) per environment, so a mistake in dev can never touch production state. Importing existing resources When something already exists in the cloud, bring it under management instead of recreating it. With current Terraform, write the target in code and add an import block: import { to = aws_s3_bucket.legacy id = \"acme-legacy-reports\" } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"legacy\" { bucket = \"acme-legacy-reports\" } Run terraform plan , which shows the import, and apply to record it. Then keep running plan and adjust the code until it reports no changes. The older command form is terraform import aws_s3_bucket.legacy acme-legacy-reports . State commands $ terraform state list $ terraform state show aws_s3_bucket.logs $ terraform state mv aws_s3_bucket.logs aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs $ terraform state rm aws_s3_bucket.legacy Command Effect state list / show Read-only inspection state mv Renames the address in state only (prefer a moved block, which is reviewable) state rm Makes Terraform forget a resource without deleting it in the cloud force-unlock ID Releases a stuck lock. Use only if you are certain no run is active Incident: \"Error acquiring the state lock\" Incident A pipeline run fails immediately with Error: Error acquiring the state lock , and the message shows a lock held by another run. Error: Error acquiring the state lock Lock Info: ID: 3f6c0a1e-... Operation: OperationTypeApply Who: ci-runner@build-17 Created: 2026-10-24 02:14:09 UTC First find out whether that run is genuinely still going. Look at your CI system for job build-17 . If it is still applying, wait : unlocking during an apply is how state gets corrupted. If the job crashed or was cancelled and clearly is not running, release the lock deliberately: $ terraform force-unlock 3f6c0a1e-... Then run terraform plan to check that state and reality agree. Prevent recurrences by allowing one run per environment at a time in the pipeline, and by never killing an apply mid-way. Predict the plan Predict first. Someone deletes a resource by hand in the console. You run terraform plan. What does"},"terraform/modules":{"html":"\u003cp>By the third environment you are copying the same hundred lines of network code and fixing the same bug in three places. A \u003cstrong>module\u003c/strong> is a folder of Terraform files used as a single building block. Every configuration is already a module (the &quot;root&quot; module). The skill is deciding what to extract and how to keep it clean.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to write and call a module\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Good interfaces: inputs, outputs and sensible defaults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning and sourcing modules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A directory layout for several environments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-module\">A module\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"modules/\n  static-site/\n    main.tf\n    variables.tf\n    outputs.tf\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">modules/\n  static-site/\n    main.tf\n    variables.tf\n    outputs.tf\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# modules/static-site/variables.tf\nvariable &quot;name&quot; {\n  type        = string\n  description = &quot;Prefix for all resource names&quot;\n}\n\nvariable &quot;tags&quot; {\n  type    = map(string)\n  default = {}\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># modules/static-site/variables.tf\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>        = string\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  description\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Prefix for all resource names&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tags&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>    = map(string)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  default\u003c/span> = {}\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# modules/static-site/main.tf\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;site&quot; {\n  bucket = &quot;${var.name}-site&quot;\n  tags   = var.tags\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block&quot; &quot;site&quot; {\n  bucket                  = aws_s3_bucket.site.id\n  block_public_acls       = true\n  block_public_policy     = true\n  ignore_public_acls      = true\n  restrict_public_buckets = true\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># modules/static-site/main.tf\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;site&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  bucket\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;${var.name}-site&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  tags\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">var\u003c/span>.tags\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;site&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  bucket\u003c/span>                  = aws_s3_bucket.site.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  block_public_acls\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  block_public_policy\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  ignore_public_acls\u003c/span>      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  restrict_public_buckets\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# modules/static-site/outputs.tf\noutput &quot;bucket_name&quot; {\n  value = aws_s3_bucket.site.bucket\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># modules/static-site/outputs.tf\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">output\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;bucket_name&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  value\u003c/span> = aws_s3_bucket.site.bucket\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Calling it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"module &quot;marketing&quot; {\n  source = &quot;../../modules/static-site&quot;\n\n  name = &quot;marketing-prod&quot;\n  tags = { Owner = &quot;web&quot; }\n}\n\noutput &quot;marketing_bucket&quot; {\n  value = module.marketing.bucket_name\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">module\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;marketing&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  source\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;../../modules/static-site&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">\n  name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;marketing-prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  tags\u003c/span> = { Owner = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span> }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">output\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;marketing_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  value\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">module\u003c/span>.marketing.bucket_name\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After adding a module call, run \u003ccode>terraform init\u003c/code> again so Terraform can load it. A module can only be read through its \u003cstrong>outputs\u003c/strong>; callers cannot reach resources inside it directly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"designing-a-good-module\">Designing a good module\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Small, clear purpose.\u003c/strong> &quot;A VPC with subnets&quot; is a module. &quot;Everything for our company&quot; is not.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Few required inputs, safe defaults.\u003c/strong> The secure choice should be the default.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Typed and described variables.\u003c/strong> The interface is the documentation.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Useful outputs.\u003c/strong> Return IDs and names that callers need to connect other things.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>No provider configuration inside.\u003c/strong> Let the caller configure providers, so the module works in any account or region.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Do not wrap a single resource without adding value.\u003c/strong> A module that just renames one resource&#39;s arguments adds complexity only.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versions-and-sources\">Versions and sources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Source\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Local path\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>source = &quot;../../modules/vpc&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Same repository, changes together\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Registry\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws&quot;\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>version = &quot;~&gt; 5.0&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Well-maintained community modules\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Internal registry or archive\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A versioned release your team publishes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shared across many teams\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always \u003cstrong>pin versions\u003c/strong> of modules you do not control, and read what a community module does before depending on it. An unpinned module can change under you on the next \u003ccode>init -upgrade\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout-for-environments\">Layout for environments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"infra/\n  modules/\n    network/\n    app/\n  envs/\n    dev/\n      main.tf\n      backend.tf\n      dev.tfvars\n    prod/\n      main.tf\n      backend.tf\n      prod.tfvars\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">infra/\n  modules/\n    network/\n    app/\n  envs/\n    dev/\n      main.tf\n      backend.tf\n      dev.tfvars\n    prod/\n      main.tf\n      backend.tf\n      prod.tfvars\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each environment is its own root module with its \u003cstrong>own state\u003c/strong>. They call the same modules with different inputs. A change to dev cannot touch production, and you promote a module change by updating the version or path in each environment in turn. Avoid using one set of files with \u003ccode>terraform workspace\u003c/code> to separate production from development. Workspaces share code and backend configuration, which makes it easy to run a command against the wrong one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-module-change-breaks-every-environment-at-once\">Incident: a module change breaks every environment at once\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A fix to the shared \u003ccode>network\u003c/code> module is merged. The next scheduled run in production proposes to replace the subnets.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The environments referenced the module by local path, so they all picked up the change immediately, and the new code renamed an internal resource. The plan flagged it before any damage:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # module.network.aws_subnet.private[0] must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 3 to add, 0 to change, 3 to destroy.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Do not apply. Revert or correct the module change, and for a rename add a \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> block inside the module so existing resources keep their identity. To avoid the class of problem, publish modules as \u003cstrong>versioned releases\u003c/strong> and let each environment upgrade deliberately: dev first, then staging, then production, reading the plan at each step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giant modules that do everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unpinned remote modules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environments that share one state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Provider blocks inside reusable modules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Renaming resources inside a module without a \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> block\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-module","text":"A module"},{"id":"designing-a-good-module","text":"Designing a good module"},{"id":"versions-and-sources","text":"Versions and sources"},{"id":"layout-for-environments","text":"Layout for environments"},{"id":"incident-a-module-change-breaks-every-environment-at-once","text":"Incident: a module change breaks every environment at once"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform: modules","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules"},{"t":"Terraform language","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language"},{"t":"Terraform: refactoring with moved blocks","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/refactoring"}],"text":"By the third environment you are copying the same hundred lines of network code and fixing the same bug in three places. A module is a folder of Terraform files used as a single building block. Every configuration is already a module (the \"root\" module). The skill is deciding what to extract and how to keep it clean. What you will learn How to write and call a module Good interfaces: inputs, outputs and sensible defaults Versioning and sourcing modules A directory layout for several environments A module modules/ static-site/ main.tf variables.tf outputs.tf # modules/static-site/variables.tf variable \"name\" { type = string description = \"Prefix for all resource names\" } variable \"tags\" { type = map(string) default = {} } # modules/static-site/main.tf resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"site\" { bucket = \"${var.name}-site\" tags = var .tags } resource \"aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block\" \"site\" { bucket = aws_s3_bucket.site.id block_public_acls = true block_public_policy = true ignore_public_acls = true restrict_public_buckets = true } # modules/static-site/outputs.tf output \"bucket_name\" { value = aws_s3_bucket.site.bucket } Calling it: module \"marketing\" { source = \"../../modules/static-site\" name = \"marketing-prod\" tags = { Owner = \"web\" } } output \"marketing_bucket\" { value = module .marketing.bucket_name } After adding a module call, run terraform init again so Terraform can load it. A module can only be read through its outputs ; callers cannot reach resources inside it directly. Designing a good module Small, clear purpose. \"A VPC with subnets\" is a module. \"Everything for our company\" is not. Few required inputs, safe defaults. The secure choice should be the default. Typed and described variables. The interface is the documentation. Useful outputs. Return IDs and names that callers need to connect other things. No provider configuration inside. Let the caller configure providers, so the module works in any account or region. Do not wrap a single resource without adding value. A module that just renames one resource&#39;s arguments adds complexity only. Versions and sources Source Example Use Local path source = \"../../modules/vpc\" Same repository, changes together Registry source = \"terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws\" with version = \"~> 5.0\" Well-maintained community modules Internal registry or archive A versioned release your team publishes Shared across many teams Always pin versions of modules you do not control, and read what a community module does before depending on it. An unpinned module can change under you on the next init -upgrade . Layout for environments infra/ modules/ network/ app/ envs/ dev/ main.tf backend.tf dev.tfvars prod/ main.tf backend.tf prod.tfvars Each environment is its own root module with its own state . They call the same modules with different inputs. A change to dev cannot touch production, and you promote a module change by updating the version or path in each environment in turn. Avoid using one set of files with terraform workspace to separate production from development. Workspaces share code and backend configuration, which makes it easy to run a command against the wrong one. Incident: a module change breaks every environment at once Incident A fix to the shared network module is merged. The next scheduled run in production proposes to replace the subnets. The environments referenced the module by local path, so they all picked up the change immediately, and the new code renamed an internal resource. The plan flagged it before any damage: # module.network.aws_subnet.private[0] must be replaced Plan: 3 to add, 0 to change, 3 to destroy. Do not apply. Revert or correct the module change, and for a rename add a moved block inside the module so existing resources keep their identity. To avoid the class of problem, publish modules as versioned releases and let each environment upgrade deliberately: dev first, then staging, then production, reading the plan at each step. Common mistakes Giant modules tha"},"terraform/secrets-and-sensitive-data":{"html":"\u003cp>Terraform needs to handle database passwords, API keys and certificates, yet its design stores a great deal in plain text. Knowing exactly where secrets can leak is the first step to preventing it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why secrets appear in state and plan output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Marking values \u003ccode>sensitive\u003c/code> and what that does and does not do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generating and fetching secrets from a secret manager\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting state, and ephemeral approaches\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-secrets-leak\">Where secrets leak\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Source code\u003c/strong>: a password written in a \u003ccode>.tf\u003c/code> file or in \u003ccode>terraform.tfvars\u003c/code> committed to version control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Plan and apply output\u003c/strong>: values shown in the console or CI log\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>State\u003c/strong>: resource attributes, including passwords you passed in, are stored in the state file in plain text\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Environment and shell history\u003c/strong>: variables exported or typed on the command line\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>sensitive = true\u003c/code> setting hides a value from CLI output, but it does \u003cstrong>not encrypt it in state\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"variable &quot;db_password&quot; {\n  type      = string\n  sensitive = true\n}\n\noutput &quot;connection_string&quot; {\n  value     = &quot;postgres://app:${var.db_password}@${aws_db_instance.main.address}/shop&quot;\n  sensitive = true\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">variable\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db_password&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  type\u003c/span>      = string\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  sensitive\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">output\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;connection_string&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  value\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgres://app:${var.db_password}@${aws_db_instance.main.address}/shop&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  sensitive\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anyone who can read the state file can read the secret. That is why state must be treated as a secret in itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"do-not-choose-passwords-in-code\">Do not choose passwords in code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Let the platform generate a password and keep it in a secret manager.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;random_password&quot; &quot;db&quot; {\n  length  = 32\n  special = false\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret&quot; &quot;db&quot; {\n  name = &quot;shop/prod/db&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret_version&quot; &quot;db&quot; {\n  secret_id     = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db.id\n  secret_string = random_password.db.result\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  identifier          = &quot;shop-prod&quot;\n  engine              = &quot;postgres&quot;\n  instance_class      = &quot;db.t3.medium&quot;\n  allocated_storage   = 50\n  username            = &quot;app&quot;\n  password            = random_password.db.result\n  skip_final_snapshot = false\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;random_password&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  length\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">32\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  special\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop/prod/db&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret_version&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  secret_id\u003c/span>     = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  secret_string\u003c/span> = random_password.db.result\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_db_instance&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  identifier\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  engine\u003c/span>              = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgres&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  instance_class\u003c/span>      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db.t3.medium&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  allocated_storage\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  username\u003c/span>            = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  password\u003c/span>            = random_password.db.result\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  skip_final_snapshot\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The password still passes through state, but it never appears in code, tickets or chat. Some resources support \u003cstrong>managed passwords\u003c/strong> where the service creates and rotates the secret itself (for example \u003ccode>manage_master_user_password = true\u003c/code> on RDS), which keeps the value out of Terraform entirely. Prefer that when available.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-existing-secrets\">Reading existing secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"data &quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret_version&quot; &quot;api_key&quot; {\n  secret_id = &quot;shop/prod/payments-api-key&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_lambda_function&quot; &quot;worker&quot; {\n  function_name = &quot;shop-worker&quot;\n  role          = aws_iam_role.worker.arn\n  runtime       = &quot;python3.12&quot;\n  handler       = &quot;main.handler&quot;\n  filename      = &quot;worker.zip&quot;\n\n  environment {\n    variables = {\n      PAYMENTS_SECRET_ARN = &quot;shop/prod/payments-api-key&quot;\n    }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">data\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret_version&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;api_key&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  secret_id\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop/prod/payments-api-key&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_lambda_function&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;worker&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  function_name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-worker&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  role\u003c/span>          = aws_iam_role.worker.arn\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  runtime\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;python3.12&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  handler\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main.handler&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  filename\u003c/span>      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;worker.zip&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n  environment {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    variables\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      PAYMENTS_SECRET_ARN\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop/prod/payments-api-key&quot;\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Notice the better pattern in the example: pass the \u003cstrong>name or ARN\u003c/strong> of the secret to the application and let the application fetch it at runtime with its IAM role. That keeps the value out of the Terraform state and out of the Lambda configuration. Reading a secret with a \u003ccode>data\u003c/code> source writes the value into state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"securing-the-state-backend\">Securing the state backend\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Store state in a remote backend with encryption at rest (for example an S3 bucket with SSE-KMS, or Azure Storage with encryption)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enable versioning so you can recover from corruption\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restrict access by IAM: only the pipeline role and a few administrators can read state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enable locking, block public access and turn on access logging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never commit \u003ccode>*.tfstate\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>*.tfvars\u003c/code> with secrets or \u003ccode>.terraform/\u003c/code> to version control\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform state pull | jq '.resources[] | select(.type==&quot;aws_db_instance&quot;) | .instances[0].attributes.password'\naws s3api get-bucket-encryption --bucket acme-tfstate\naws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket acme-tfstate\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state pull | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.resources[] | select(.type==&quot;aws_db_instance&quot;) | .instances[0].attributes.password&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-encryption --bucket acme-tfstate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket acme-tfstate\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The second command demonstrates the risk: reading the state exposes the database password.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"supplying-inputs-in-a-pipeline\">Supplying inputs in a pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"export TF_VAR_db_password=&quot;$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id shop/prod/db --query SecretString --output text)&quot;\nterraform plan -out=tfplan\nterraform apply tfplan\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> TF_VAR_db_password=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id shop/prod/db --query SecretString --output text)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan -out=tfplan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform apply tfplan\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Plan files can also contain secrets, so treat them as sensitive and delete them after use. CI systems should inject variables from a protected store and mask them in logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-password-in-the-pull-request\">Incident: the password in the pull request\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A developer adds \u003ccode>password = &quot;S3cret!2024&quot;\u003c/code> to a Terraform file and opens a pull request. The branch is pushed, the CI log prints the plan, and the password appears in the review tool, the build log and the plan artifact.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Treat the password as compromised. Rotate it first, then clean up:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws rds modify-db-instance --db-instance-identifier shop-prod --manage-master-user-password --apply-immediately\naws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id 'rds!db-abc123' --query SecretString --output text | head -c 0\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws rds modify-db-instance --db-instance-identifier shop-prod --manage-master-user-password --apply-immediately\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rds!db-abc123&#x27;\u003c/span> --query SecretString --output text | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After rotation, remove the literal from code, mark the variable \u003ccode>sensitive\u003c/code>, delete or redact the log and artifact, and add a pre-commit secret scanner plus a pipeline check that fails when credentials patterns appear. Prefer managed passwords so there is nothing to commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Believing \u003ccode>sensitive = true\u003c/code> encrypts the state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Committing \u003ccode>terraform.tfvars\u003c/code> or state files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading secrets with data sources and passing them into resource arguments unnecessarily\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the state bucket readable by many people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping old plan files that contain secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-secrets-leak","text":"Where secrets leak"},{"id":"do-not-choose-passwords-in-code","text":"Do not choose passwords in code"},{"id":"reading-existing-secrets","text":"Reading existing secrets"},{"id":"securing-the-state-backend","text":"Securing the state backend"},{"id":"supplying-inputs-in-a-pipeline","text":"Supplying inputs in a pipeline"},{"id":"incident-the-password-in-the-pull-request","text":"Incident: the password in the pull request"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform: sensitive data in state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state/sensitive-data"},{"t":"Terraform: input variables","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/variables"},{"t":"Azure Key Vault","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/overview"}],"text":"Terraform needs to handle database passwords, API keys and certificates, yet its design stores a great deal in plain text. Knowing exactly where secrets can leak is the first step to preventing it. What you will learn Why secrets appear in state and plan output Marking values sensitive and what that does and does not do Generating and fetching secrets from a secret manager Protecting state, and ephemeral approaches Where secrets leak Source code : a password written in a .tf file or in terraform.tfvars committed to version control Plan and apply output : values shown in the console or CI log State : resource attributes, including passwords you passed in, are stored in the state file in plain text Environment and shell history : variables exported or typed on the command line The sensitive = true setting hides a value from CLI output, but it does not encrypt it in state . variable \"db_password\" { type = string sensitive = true } output \"connection_string\" { value = \"postgres://app:${var.db_password}@${aws_db_instance.main.address}/shop\" sensitive = true } Anyone who can read the state file can read the secret. That is why state must be treated as a secret in itself. Do not choose passwords in code Let the platform generate a password and keep it in a secret manager. resource \"random_password\" \"db\" { length = 32 special = false } resource \"aws_secretsmanager_secret\" \"db\" { name = \"shop/prod/db\" } resource \"aws_secretsmanager_secret_version\" \"db\" { secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db.id secret_string = random_password.db.result } resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" { identifier = \"shop-prod\" engine = \"postgres\" instance_class = \"db.t3.medium\" allocated_storage = 50 username = \"app\" password = random_password.db.result skip_final_snapshot = false } The password still passes through state, but it never appears in code, tickets or chat. Some resources support managed passwords where the service creates and rotates the secret itself (for example manage_master_user_password = true on RDS), which keeps the value out of Terraform entirely. Prefer that when available. Reading existing secrets data \"aws_secretsmanager_secret_version\" \"api_key\" { secret_id = \"shop/prod/payments-api-key\" } resource \"aws_lambda_function\" \"worker\" { function_name = \"shop-worker\" role = aws_iam_role.worker.arn runtime = \"python3.12\" handler = \"main.handler\" filename = \"worker.zip\" environment { variables = { PAYMENTS_SECRET_ARN = \"shop/prod/payments-api-key\" } } } Notice the better pattern in the example: pass the name or ARN of the secret to the application and let the application fetch it at runtime with its IAM role. That keeps the value out of the Terraform state and out of the Lambda configuration. Reading a secret with a data source writes the value into state. Securing the state backend Store state in a remote backend with encryption at rest (for example an S3 bucket with SSE-KMS, or Azure Storage with encryption) Enable versioning so you can recover from corruption Restrict access by IAM: only the pipeline role and a few administrators can read state Enable locking, block public access and turn on access logging Never commit *.tfstate , *.tfvars with secrets or .terraform/ to version control $ terraform state pull | jq &#x27;.resources[] | select(.type==\"aws_db_instance\") | .instances[0].attributes.password&#x27; $ aws s3api get-bucket-encryption --bucket acme-tfstate $ aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket acme-tfstate The second command demonstrates the risk: reading the state exposes the database password. Supplying inputs in a pipeline $ export TF_VAR_db_password= \" $(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id shop/prod/db --query SecretString --output text) \" $ terraform plan -out=tfplan $ terraform apply tfplan Plan files can also contain secrets, so treat them as sensitive and delete them after use. CI systems should inject variables from a protected store and mask them in logs. Incident: the password in the pull request Incident A "},"terraform/aws-and-azure-end-to-end":{"html":"\u003cp>Concepts become clear when you build something. This lesson assembles a small, realistic stack on both clouds: a private network, a load balancer, application servers and a managed database. The resources differ in name, but the design is the same.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A reference layout: public edge, private application tier, private data tier\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The AWS resources and their Azure equivalents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to structure the project, remote state and variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A safe plan, apply and destroy routine\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-design\">The design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Network\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VPC, subnets, route tables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Virtual network, subnets, route tables\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Internet access\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Internet gateway, NAT gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Public IP, NAT gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Traffic filtering\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Security groups\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Network security groups\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Edge\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application Load Balancer\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application Gateway or Load Balancer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Compute\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EC2 or Auto Scaling group\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Virtual machine scale set\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Database\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>RDS PostgreSQL\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Identity\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>IAM role on the instance\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Managed identity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Only the load balancer is public. Application servers sit in private subnets and the database in its own subnets, reachable only from the application tier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"project-layout\">Project layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"infra/\n  aws/\n    main.tf\n    variables.tf\n    outputs.tf\n    backend.tf\n  azure/\n    main.tf\n    variables.tf\n    outputs.tf\n    backend.tf\n  modules/\n    network/\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">infra/\n  aws/\n    main.tf\n    variables.tf\n    outputs.tf\n    backend.tf\n  azure/\n    main.tf\n    variables.tf\n    outputs.tf\n    backend.tf\n  modules/\n    network/\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keep each cloud in its own root module with its own state. Do not try to make one configuration create both: the providers, authentication and lifecycle differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"aws-network-and-compute\">AWS network and compute\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform {\n  required_version = &quot;&gt;= 1.6&quot;\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = &quot;hashicorp/aws&quot;\n      version = &quot;~&gt; 5.0&quot;\n    }\n  }\n  backend &quot;s3&quot; {\n    bucket         = &quot;acme-tfstate&quot;\n    key            = &quot;shop/aws/terraform.tfstate&quot;\n    region         = &quot;eu-west-1&quot;\n    dynamodb_table = &quot;tf-locks&quot;\n    encrypt        = true\n  }\n}\n\nprovider &quot;aws&quot; {\n  region = &quot;eu-west-1&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  cidr_block           = &quot;10.10.0.0/16&quot;\n  enable_dns_hostnames = true\n  tags                 = { Name = &quot;shop&quot; }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_subnet&quot; &quot;private&quot; {\n  count             = 2\n  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id\n  cidr_block        = cidrsubnet(aws_vpc.main.cidr_block, 8, count.index + 10)\n  availability_zone = data.aws_availability_zones.available.names[count.index]\n}\n\ndata &quot;aws_availability_zones&quot; &quot;available&quot; {\n  state = &quot;available&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_security_group&quot; &quot;app&quot; {\n  name   = &quot;shop-app&quot;\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  ingress {\n    from_port       = 8080\n    to_port         = 8080\n    protocol        = &quot;tcp&quot;\n    security_groups = [aws_security_group.alb.id]\n  }\n\n  egress {\n    from_port   = 0\n    to_port     = 0\n    protocol    = &quot;-1&quot;\n    cidr_blocks = [&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;]\n  }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_security_group&quot; &quot;alb&quot; {\n  name   = &quot;shop-alb&quot;\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = &quot;tcp&quot;\n    cidr_blocks = [&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;]\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">terraform\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  required_version\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&gt;= 1.6&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">required_providers\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    aws\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      source\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hashicorp/aws&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      version\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;~&gt; 5.0&quot;\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">backend\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    bucket\u003c/span>         = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-tfstate&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    key\u003c/span>            = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop/aws/terraform.tfstate&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    region\u003c/span>         = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    dynamodb_table\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tf-locks&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    encrypt\u003c/span>        = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">provider\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  region\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eu-west-1&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_vpc&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  cidr_block\u003c/span>           = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.10.0.0/16&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  enable_dns_hostnames\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  tags\u003c/span>                 = { Name = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop&quot;\u003c/span> }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_subnet&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;private&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  count\u003c/span>             = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span>            = aws_vpc.main.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  cidr_block\u003c/span>        = cidrsubnet(aws_vpc.main.cidr_block, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>.index + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  availability_zone\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">data\u003c/span>.aws_availability_zones.available.names[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>.index]\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">data\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_availability_zones&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;available&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  state\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;available&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_security_group&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-app&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span> = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  ingress {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    from_port\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    to_port\u003c/span>         = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    protocol\u003c/span>        = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tcp&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    security_groups\u003c/span> = [aws_security_group.alb.id]\n  }\n\n  egress {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    from_port\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    to_port\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    protocol\u003c/span>    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-1&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    cidr_blocks\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_security_group&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;alb&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-alb&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span> = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  ingress {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    from_port\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    to_port\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    protocol\u003c/span>    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tcp&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    cidr_blocks\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The application security group allows traffic \u003cstrong>only from the load balancer&#39;s security group\u003c/strong>, not from an address range. That is the AWS idiom for tier-to-tier rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"azure-network-and-compute\">Azure network and compute\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform {\n  required_providers {\n    azurerm = {\n      source  = &quot;hashicorp/azurerm&quot;\n      version = &quot;~&gt; 3.100&quot;\n    }\n  }\n  backend &quot;azurerm&quot; {\n    resource_group_name  = &quot;rg-tfstate&quot;\n    storage_account_name = &quot;acmetfstate&quot;\n    container_name       = &quot;tfstate&quot;\n    key                  = &quot;shop/azure.tfstate&quot;\n  }\n}\n\nprovider &quot;azurerm&quot; {\n  features {}\n}\n\nresource &quot;azurerm_resource_group&quot; &quot;shop&quot; {\n  name     = &quot;rg-shop-prod&quot;\n  location = &quot;westeurope&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;azurerm_virtual_network&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  name                = &quot;vnet-shop&quot;\n  address_space       = [&quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;]\n  location            = azurerm_resource_group.shop.location\n  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name\n}\n\nresource &quot;azurerm_subnet&quot; &quot;app&quot; {\n  name                 = &quot;snet-app&quot;\n  resource_group_name  = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name\n  virtual_network_name = azurerm_virtual_network.main.name\n  address_prefixes     = [&quot;10.20.10.0/24&quot;]\n}\n\nresource &quot;azurerm_network_security_group&quot; &quot;app&quot; {\n  name                = &quot;nsg-app&quot;\n  location            = azurerm_resource_group.shop.location\n  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name\n\n  security_rule {\n    name                       = &quot;allow-lb-8080&quot;\n    priority                   = 100\n    direction                  = &quot;Inbound&quot;\n    access                     = &quot;Allow&quot;\n    protocol                   = &quot;Tcp&quot;\n    source_port_range          = &quot;*&quot;\n    destination_port_range     = &quot;8080&quot;\n    source_address_prefix      = &quot;AzureLoadBalancer&quot;\n    destination_address_prefix = &quot;*&quot;\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">terraform\u003c/span> {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">required_providers\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    azurerm\u003c/span> = {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      source\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hashicorp/azurerm&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">      version\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;~&gt; 3.100&quot;\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">backend\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;azurerm&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    resource_group_name\u003c/span>  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rg-tfstate&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    storage_account_name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acmetfstate&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    container_name\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tfstate&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    key\u003c/span>                  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop/azure.tfstate&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">provider\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;azurerm&quot;\u003c/span> {\n  features {}\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;azurerm_resource_group&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rg-shop-prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  location\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;westeurope&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;azurerm_virtual_network&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>                = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vnet-shop&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  address_space\u003c/span>       = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.20.0.0/16&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  location\u003c/span>            = azurerm_resource_group.shop.location\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  resource_group_name\u003c/span> = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;azurerm_subnet&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>                 = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;snet-app&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  resource_group_name\u003c/span>  = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  virtual_network_name\u003c/span> = azurerm_virtual_network.main.name\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  address_prefixes\u003c/span>     = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.20.10.0/24&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;azurerm_network_security_group&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>                = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nsg-app&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  location\u003c/span>            = azurerm_resource_group.shop.location\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  resource_group_name\u003c/span> = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name\n\n  security_rule {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    name\u003c/span>                       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;allow-lb-8080&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    priority\u003c/span>                   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    direction\u003c/span>                  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Inbound&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    access\u003c/span>                     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Allow&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    protocol\u003c/span>                   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Tcp&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    source_port_range\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    destination_port_range\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;8080&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    source_address_prefix\u003c/span>      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AzureLoadBalancer&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    destination_address_prefix\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Azure resources live in a \u003cstrong>resource group\u003c/strong>, which acts as a lifecycle and access boundary. Network security groups use numbered priorities where the lowest number wins.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-it-safely\">Running it safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cd infra/aws\nterraform init\nterraform fmt -check &amp;&amp; terraform validate\nterraform plan -out=tfplan\nterraform show tfplan | head -40\nterraform apply tfplan\nterraform output\nterraform destroy -target=aws_instance.test\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> infra/aws\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform init\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">fmt\u003c/span> -check &amp;&amp; terraform validate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan -out=tfplan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform show tfplan | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -40\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform apply tfplan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform output\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.test\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read the plan every time. Look for \u003ccode>-/+\u003c/code> (replace) and \u003ccode>destroy\u003c/code> lines on stateful resources such as databases. Use \u003ccode>lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }\u003c/code> on the database and enable deletion protection. Tag everything with owner and environment so cost reports make sense.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-apply-that-replaced-the-database\">Incident: the apply that replaced the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change renames an RDS argument. The plan shows \u003ccode>aws_db_instance.main must be replaced\u003c/code>, but the engineer scrolls past it in a 400-line plan, approves it, and the database is destroyed and recreated empty.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Prevention starts in code and process:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  identifier              = &quot;shop-prod&quot;\n  engine                  = &quot;postgres&quot;\n  instance_class          = &quot;db.t3.medium&quot;\n  allocated_storage       = 50\n  username                = &quot;app&quot;\n  manage_master_user_password = true\n  deletion_protection     = true\n  backup_retention_period = 14\n  skip_final_snapshot     = false\n  final_snapshot_identifier = &quot;shop-prod-final&quot;\n\n  lifecycle {\n    prevent_destroy = true\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_db_instance&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  identifier\u003c/span>              = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  engine\u003c/span>                  = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgres&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  instance_class\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db.t3.medium&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  allocated_storage\u003c/span>       = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  username\u003c/span>                = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  manage_master_user_password\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  deletion_protection\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  backup_retention_period\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  skip_final_snapshot\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  final_snapshot_identifier\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-prod-final&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lifecycle\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    prevent_destroy\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>prevent_destroy\u003c/code> the plan itself errors instead of destroying. Recover from the automated backups or the final snapshot, add a CI step that fails when a plan contains a replacement of a protected resource, and require a second reviewer for any plan that destroys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One giant state for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wide-open security groups and storage accounts for convenience\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not pinning provider versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying a plan different from the one reviewed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that destroy is permanent for databases without snapshots\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-design","text":"The design"},{"id":"project-layout","text":"Project layout"},{"id":"aws-network-and-compute","text":"AWS network and compute"},{"id":"azure-network-and-compute","text":"Azure network and compute"},{"id":"running-it-safely","text":"Running it safely"},{"id":"incident-the-apply-that-replaced-the-database","text":"Incident: the apply that replaced the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform AWS provider","u":"https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs"},{"t":"Terraform AzureRM provider","u":"https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/azurerm/latest/docs"},{"t":"Terraform: lifecycle meta-argument","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/meta-arguments/lifecycle"}],"text":"Concepts become clear when you build something. This lesson assembles a small, realistic stack on both clouds: a private network, a load balancer, application servers and a managed database. The resources differ in name, but the design is the same. What you will learn A reference layout: public edge, private application tier, private data tier The AWS resources and their Azure equivalents How to structure the project, remote state and variables A safe plan, apply and destroy routine The design Layer AWS Azure Network VPC, subnets, route tables Virtual network, subnets, route tables Internet access Internet gateway, NAT gateway Public IP, NAT gateway Traffic filtering Security groups Network security groups Edge Application Load Balancer Application Gateway or Load Balancer Compute EC2 or Auto Scaling group Virtual machine scale set Database RDS PostgreSQL Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server Identity IAM role on the instance Managed identity Only the load balancer is public. Application servers sit in private subnets and the database in its own subnets, reachable only from the application tier. Project layout infra/ aws/ main.tf variables.tf outputs.tf backend.tf azure/ main.tf variables.tf outputs.tf backend.tf modules/ network/ Keep each cloud in its own root module with its own state. Do not try to make one configuration create both: the providers, authentication and lifecycle differ. AWS network and compute terraform { required_version = \">= 1.6\" required_providers { aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\" version = \"~> 5.0\" } } backend \"s3\" { bucket = \"acme-tfstate\" key = \"shop/aws/terraform.tfstate\" region = \"eu-west-1\" dynamodb_table = \"tf-locks\" encrypt = true } } provider \"aws\" { region = \"eu-west-1\" } resource \"aws_vpc\" \"main\" { cidr_block = \"10.10.0.0/16\" enable_dns_hostnames = true tags = { Name = \"shop\" } } resource \"aws_subnet\" \"private\" { count = 2 vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id cidr_block = cidrsubnet(aws_vpc.main.cidr_block, 8 , count .index + 10 ) availability_zone = data .aws_availability_zones.available.names[ count .index] } data \"aws_availability_zones\" \"available\" { state = \"available\" } resource \"aws_security_group\" \"app\" { name = \"shop-app\" vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id ingress { from_port = 8080 to_port = 8080 protocol = \"tcp\" security_groups = [aws_security_group.alb.id] } egress { from_port = 0 to_port = 0 protocol = \"-1\" cidr_blocks = [ \"0.0.0.0/0\" ] } } resource \"aws_security_group\" \"alb\" { name = \"shop-alb\" vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id ingress { from_port = 443 to_port = 443 protocol = \"tcp\" cidr_blocks = [ \"0.0.0.0/0\" ] } } The application security group allows traffic only from the load balancer&#39;s security group , not from an address range. That is the AWS idiom for tier-to-tier rules. Azure network and compute terraform { required_providers { azurerm = { source = \"hashicorp/azurerm\" version = \"~> 3.100\" } } backend \"azurerm\" { resource_group_name = \"rg-tfstate\" storage_account_name = \"acmetfstate\" container_name = \"tfstate\" key = \"shop/azure.tfstate\" } } provider \"azurerm\" { features {} } resource \"azurerm_resource_group\" \"shop\" { name = \"rg-shop-prod\" location = \"westeurope\" } resource \"azurerm_virtual_network\" \"main\" { name = \"vnet-shop\" address_space = [ \"10.20.0.0/16\" ] location = azurerm_resource_group.shop.location resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name } resource \"azurerm_subnet\" \"app\" { name = \"snet-app\" resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name virtual_network_name = azurerm_virtual_network.main.name address_prefixes = [ \"10.20.10.0/24\" ] } resource \"azurerm_network_security_group\" \"app\" { name = \"nsg-app\" location = azurerm_resource_group.shop.location resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name security_rule { name = \"allow-lb-8080\" priority = 100 direction = \"Inbound\" access = \"Allow\" protocol = \"Tcp\" source_port_range = \"*\" destination_port_range = \"8080\" source_address_prefix = \"AzureLoadBalancer\" destination_address_prefix = \"*\" } } Azure resources"},"terraform/workflows-and-drift":{"html":"\u003cp>Running \u003ccode>terraform apply\u003c/code> from a laptop works until the second engineer joins. Then you need a shared workflow: changes reviewed before they run, one place where applies happen, and a way to notice when reality has wandered away from the code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A pipeline pattern for plan and apply\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Detecting and handling drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Static checks and tests before apply\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe handling of destructive changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-pipeline-pattern\">The pipeline pattern\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>A change is proposed and a pipeline runs \u003ccode>fmt -check\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>validate\u003c/code> and security checks.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The pipeline runs \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> and posts the result for review.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A human approves the plan and the code together.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>After approval, the pipeline runs \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> with the \u003cstrong>saved\u003c/strong> plan, from a single controlled environment.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform fmt -check -recursive\nterraform init -input=false\nterraform validate\nterraform plan -input=false -out=tfplan\n# review step happens here\nterraform apply -input=false tfplan\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">terraform \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">fmt\u003c/span> -check -recursive\nterraform init -input=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\nterraform validate\nterraform plan -input=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span> -out=tfplan\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># review step happens here\u003c/span>\nterraform apply -input=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span> tfplan\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pipeline should authenticate with short-lived credentials (a role assumed through the CI system&#39;s identity federation) and not long-lived keys stored as variables. Give plan jobs read-only permission and apply jobs the write permission, so a plan on an untrusted change cannot modify anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drift\">Drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Drift\u003c/strong> means the real infrastructure no longer matches the code. It happens when someone changes something in the console, when another tool modifies a resource, or when a cloud service changes a default.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan -detailed-exitcode\necho $?\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan -detailed-exitcode\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> $?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>-detailed-exitcode\u003c/code>, the exit status is \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> for no changes, \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> for an error, and \u003ccode>2\u003c/code> when changes are present. Run this on a schedule for each environment and alert on \u003ccode>2\u003c/code> where nothing was supposed to change. When you find drift, decide which side is right:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>The manual change was a mistake\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> to put the real resource back to the code\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>The manual change was needed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Update the code to match, then confirm \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> is clean\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>The attribute is managed elsewhere (an autoscaler changes the count)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Add \u003ccode>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [...] }\u003c/code> for that field only\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>terraform apply -refresh-only\u003c/code> updates state to match reality without changing infrastructure, useful when you want to record a change that is already correct.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checks-before-apply\">Checks before apply\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Check\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Catches\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform fmt -check\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Style inconsistencies\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform validate\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Syntax and reference errors\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tflint\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Provider-specific mistakes such as invalid instance types\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>trivy config .\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>checkov\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Insecure settings such as open security groups and unencrypted storage\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform test\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Behaviour of modules against assertions you write\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Policy checks (for example OPA)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Organization rules, such as required tags\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A minimal native test file:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# tests/bucket.tftest.hcl\nrun &quot;bucket_is_private&quot; {\n  command = plan\n\n  variables {\n    name = &quot;test-site&quot;\n  }\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.site.block_public_acls == true\n    error_message = &quot;Public ACLs must be blocked.&quot;\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># tests/bucket.tftest.hcl\u003c/span>\nrun \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;bucket_is_private&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  command\u003c/span> = plan\n\n  variables {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;test-site&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n\n  assert {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    condition\u003c/span>     = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.site.block_public_acls == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    error_message\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Public ACLs must be blocked.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"destructive-changes\">Destructive changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Treat these as separate, deliberate changes: replacing databases, deleting storage, and changing network ranges. Use \u003ccode>prevent_destroy\u003c/code>, take backups or snapshots first, apply in a quiet period, and confirm the plan lists exactly the resources you expect. For zero-downtime replacement of stateless resources, \u003ccode>create_before_destroy\u003c/code> builds the new one first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-scheduled-plan-shows-unexpected-changes\">Incident: scheduled plan shows unexpected changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Your nightly drift check exits with \u003ccode>2\u003c/code> and the plan wants to remove a security group rule that nobody in the team touched.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_security_group.web will be updated in-place\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  ~ resource &quot;aws_security_group&quot; &quot;web&quot; {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      ~ ingress = [\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">          - {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">              - cidr_blocks = [&quot;203.0.113.10/32&quot;]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">              - description = &quot;temp debug access&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">              - from_port   = 22\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone added an SSH rule in the console during an investigation. The drift check found it. Do not just apply and move on. Ask who added it and why, since an incident may still be in progress. If the access is no longer needed, apply to remove it, which restores the intended state and closes an unreviewed hole. If it is needed, add it to the code through a reviewed change. Then reduce future console edits: restrict write permission in the console to a break-glass role, and make that role&#39;s use alert the team.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pr&gt;plan;plan&gt;rev;rev&gt;app;app&gt;drift;drift&gt;pr\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A safe infrastructure change\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"12.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pull request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Apply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drift\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Drift check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pull request to Plan; 2, Plan to Review; 3, Review to Apply; 4, Apply to Drift check; 5, Drift check to Pull request.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pr\">\u003cdt>Pull request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Code change and a plan comment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without a plan, reviewers guess.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows adds, changes and destroys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A destroy of a database must stop everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A person checks the plan, not only the code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rubber-stamping misses surprises.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Apply \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Applies the saved plan from the pipeline.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Applying from a laptop skips audit and locking.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drift\">\u003cdt>Drift check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A scheduled plan finds manual changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unnoticed drift makes the next apply risky.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Applying a fresh plan that differs from the one reviewed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long-lived cloud keys stored in CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never checking for drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling a failing security check instead of fixing the finding\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-pipeline-pattern","text":"The pipeline pattern"},{"id":"drift","text":"Drift"},{"id":"checks-before-apply","text":"Checks before apply"},{"id":"destructive-changes","text":"Destructive changes"},{"id":"incident-scheduled-plan-shows-unexpected-changes","text":"Incident: scheduled plan shows unexpected changes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform CLI","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli"},{"t":"Terraform: tests","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/tests"},{"t":"Terraform: state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state"}],"text":"Running terraform apply from a laptop works until the second engineer joins. Then you need a shared workflow: changes reviewed before they run, one place where applies happen, and a way to notice when reality has wandered away from the code. What you will learn A pipeline pattern for plan and apply Detecting and handling drift Static checks and tests before apply Safe handling of destructive changes The pipeline pattern A change is proposed and a pipeline runs fmt -check , validate and security checks. The pipeline runs plan and posts the result for review. A human approves the plan and the code together. After approval, the pipeline runs apply with the saved plan, from a single controlled environment. terraform fmt -check -recursive terraform init -input= false terraform validate terraform plan -input= false -out=tfplan # review step happens here terraform apply -input= false tfplan The pipeline should authenticate with short-lived credentials (a role assumed through the CI system&#39;s identity federation) and not long-lived keys stored as variables. Give plan jobs read-only permission and apply jobs the write permission, so a plan on an untrusted change cannot modify anything. Drift Drift means the real infrastructure no longer matches the code. It happens when someone changes something in the console, when another tool modifies a resource, or when a cloud service changes a default. $ terraform plan -detailed-exitcode $ echo $? 2 With -detailed-exitcode , the exit status is 0 for no changes, 1 for an error, and 2 when changes are present. Run this on a schedule for each environment and alert on 2 where nothing was supposed to change. When you find drift, decide which side is right: Situation Action The manual change was a mistake apply to put the real resource back to the code The manual change was needed Update the code to match, then confirm plan is clean The attribute is managed elsewhere (an autoscaler changes the count) Add lifecycle { ignore_changes = [...] } for that field only terraform apply -refresh-only updates state to match reality without changing infrastructure, useful when you want to record a change that is already correct. Checks before apply Check Catches terraform fmt -check Style inconsistencies terraform validate Syntax and reference errors tflint Provider-specific mistakes such as invalid instance types trivy config . or checkov Insecure settings such as open security groups and unencrypted storage terraform test Behaviour of modules against assertions you write Policy checks (for example OPA) Organization rules, such as required tags A minimal native test file: # tests/bucket.tftest.hcl run \"bucket_is_private\" { command = plan variables { name = \"test-site\" } assert { condition = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.site.block_public_acls == true error_message = \"Public ACLs must be blocked.\" } } Destructive changes Treat these as separate, deliberate changes: replacing databases, deleting storage, and changing network ranges. Use prevent_destroy , take backups or snapshots first, apply in a quiet period, and confirm the plan lists exactly the resources you expect. For zero-downtime replacement of stateless resources, create_before_destroy builds the new one first. Incident: scheduled plan shows unexpected changes Incident Your nightly drift check exits with 2 and the plan wants to remove a security group rule that nobody in the team touched. # aws_security_group.web will be updated in-place ~ resource \"aws_security_group\" \"web\" { ~ ingress = [ - { - cidr_blocks = [\"203.0.113.10/32\"] - description = \"temp debug access\" - from_port = 22 Someone added an SSH rule in the console during an investigation. The drift check found it. Do not just apply and move on. Ask who added it and why, since an incident may still be in progress. If the access is no longer needed, apply to remove it, which restores the intended state and closes an unreviewed hole. If it is needed, add it to the code through a reviewed change"},"terraform/testing-terraform":{"html":"\u003cp>Infrastructure code changes can delete databases, open networks to the world or cost a lot of money. Yet many teams test it only by applying it and watching. A layered approach finds most mistakes in seconds, before anything is created, and keeps expensive tests for the cases that need them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The testing layers from cheap to expensive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>fmt\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>validate\u003c/code>, linters and security scanners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policy checks on the plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>terraform test\u003c/code> with mocks and real runs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping test environments cheap and clean\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layers\">Layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Finds\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Format\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform fmt -check\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Style differences\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Validate\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform validate\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Syntax, wrong references, wrong types\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Lint\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tflint\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Invalid instance types, deprecated syntax, provider rules\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Security scan\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Trivy, Checkov or similar\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Public buckets, open ports, missing encryption\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Plan checks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Policy as code on plan JSON\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rules such as required tags, no destroys of tagged resources\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds to a minute\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Unit tests\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform test\u003c/code> with mocks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Module logic and outputs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Integration tests\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform test\u003c/code> or Terratest with real resources\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Whether it really works\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minutes, small cost\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cheap-layers-in-a-pipeline\">The cheap layers in a pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform fmt -check -recursive\nterraform init -backend=false\nterraform validate\ntflint --recursive\ntrivy config .\nterraform plan -out=tfplan &amp;&amp; terraform show -json tfplan &gt; plan.json\nconftest test plan.json --policy policy/\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">terraform \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">fmt\u003c/span> -check -recursive\nterraform init -backend=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\nterraform validate\ntflint --recursive\ntrivy config .\nterraform plan -out=tfplan &amp;&amp; terraform show -json tfplan &gt; plan.json\nconftest \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> plan.json --policy policy/\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run these on every change. They need no cloud credentials for most steps (use \u003ccode>-backend=false\u003c/code> and, for the plan, a read-only role).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"terraform-test\">terraform test\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Terraform has a built-in test framework with files that end in \u003ccode>.tftest.hcl\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# tests/bucket.tftest.hcl\nvariables {\n  name = &quot;test-logs&quot;\n}\n\nrun &quot;creates_private_encrypted_bucket&quot; {\n  command = plan\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.this.block_public_acls == true\n    error_message = &quot;public ACLs must be blocked&quot;\n  }\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = length(aws_s3_bucket.this.tags) &gt;= 2\n    error_message = &quot;bucket must carry owner and environment tags&quot;\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># tests/bucket.tftest.hcl\u003c/span>\nvariables {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;test-logs&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\nrun \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;creates_private_encrypted_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  command\u003c/span> = plan\n\n  assert {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    condition\u003c/span>     = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.this.block_public_acls == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    error_message\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;public ACLs must be blocked&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n\n  assert {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    condition\u003c/span>     = length(aws_s3_bucket.this.tags) &gt;= \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    error_message\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;bucket must carry owner and environment tags&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>command = plan\u003c/code>, nothing is created. With \u003ccode>command = apply\u003c/code> (the default), Terraform creates real resources in the test and destroys them at the end. Mocked providers let you test module logic without any cloud calls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"integration-tests\">Integration tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a separate account or project with budget alerts, unique names (random suffix) so tests can run in parallel, and a clean-up job that removes leftovers older than a few hours. Go-based Terratest can also check behaviour, such as making an HTTP request to the new load balancer. Test modules that are used widely; do not apply a full production stack on every commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-assert\">What to assert\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Security: encryption on, public access off, least-privilege policies.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Contracts: a module&#39;s outputs exist and have the expected shape.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safety: a plan for a change to production does not destroy tagged data stores.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defaults: a module works with only the required inputs.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Which test layer catches a reference to a variable that does not exist?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> terraform validate, which checks syntax and references without creating anything.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The cheapest layer finds many errors. Run it first and on every change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-module-that-opened-the-database\">Incident: the module that opened the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A module update makes a database reachable from the internet in a staging account. It is found two weeks later by an external scan.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"conftest test plan.json --policy policy/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>conftest \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> plan.json --policy policy/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FAIL - plan.json - aws_security_group.db: ingress from 0.0.0.0/0 is not allowed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new default for a variable had changed the allowed range, and no test examined the plan. The team added a plan policy that rejects public ingress to data stores, a \u003ccode>terraform test\u003c/code> for the module defaults, and made the checks required for merging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Only testing by applying to production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tests that leave resources behind\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Integration tests on every commit in an expensive account\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policy checks that can be skipped by developers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layers","text":"Layers"},{"id":"the-cheap-layers-in-a-pipeline","text":"The cheap layers in a pipeline"},{"id":"terraform-test","text":"terraform test"},{"id":"integration-tests","text":"Integration tests"},{"id":"what-to-assert","text":"What to assert"},{"id":"incident-the-module-that-opened-the-database","text":"Incident: the module that opened the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform docs: Tests","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/tests"},{"t":"Terraform docs: terraform validate","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/validate"}],"text":"Infrastructure code changes can delete databases, open networks to the world or cost a lot of money. Yet many teams test it only by applying it and watching. A layered approach finds most mistakes in seconds, before anything is created, and keeps expensive tests for the cases that need them. What you will learn The testing layers from cheap to expensive fmt , validate , linters and security scanners Policy checks on the plan terraform test with mocks and real runs Keeping test environments cheap and clean Layers Layer Tool Finds Cost Format terraform fmt -check Style differences Seconds Validate terraform validate Syntax, wrong references, wrong types Seconds Lint tflint Invalid instance types, deprecated syntax, provider rules Seconds Security scan Trivy, Checkov or similar Public buckets, open ports, missing encryption Seconds Plan checks Policy as code on plan JSON Rules such as required tags, no destroys of tagged resources Seconds to a minute Unit tests terraform test with mocks Module logic and outputs Seconds Integration tests terraform test or Terratest with real resources Whether it really works Minutes, small cost The cheap layers in a pipeline terraform fmt -check -recursive terraform init -backend= false terraform validate tflint --recursive trivy config . terraform plan -out=tfplan && terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json conftest test plan.json --policy policy/ Run these on every change. They need no cloud credentials for most steps (use -backend=false and, for the plan, a read-only role). terraform test Terraform has a built-in test framework with files that end in .tftest.hcl : # tests/bucket.tftest.hcl variables { name = \"test-logs\" } run \"creates_private_encrypted_bucket\" { command = plan assert { condition = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.this.block_public_acls == true error_message = \"public ACLs must be blocked\" } assert { condition = length(aws_s3_bucket.this.tags) >= 2 error_message = \"bucket must carry owner and environment tags\" } } With command = plan , nothing is created. With command = apply (the default), Terraform creates real resources in the test and destroys them at the end. Mocked providers let you test module logic without any cloud calls. Integration tests Use a separate account or project with budget alerts, unique names (random suffix) so tests can run in parallel, and a clean-up job that removes leftovers older than a few hours. Go-based Terratest can also check behaviour, such as making an HTTP request to the new load balancer. Test modules that are used widely; do not apply a full production stack on every commit. What to assert Security: encryption on, public access off, least-privilege policies. Contracts: a module&#39;s outputs exist and have the expected shape. Safety: a plan for a change to production does not destroy tagged data stores. Defaults: a module works with only the required inputs. Predict first. Which test layer catches a reference to a variable that does not exist? Your guess Answer: terraform validate, which checks syntax and references without creating anything. The cheapest layer finds many errors. Run it first and on every change. Incident: the module that opened the database Incident A module update makes a database reachable from the internet in a staging account. It is found two weeks later by an external scan. $ conftest test plan.json --policy policy/ FAIL - plan.json - aws_security_group.db: ingress from 0.0.0.0/0 is not allowed The new default for a variable had changed the allowed range, and no test examined the plan. The team added a plan policy that rejects public ingress to data stores, a terraform test for the module defaults, and made the checks required for merging. Common mistakes Only testing by applying to production Tests that leave resources behind Integration tests on every commit in an expensive account Policy checks that can be skipped by developers"},"terraform/environments-and-team-workflows":{"html":"\u003cp>A Terraform setup that works for one person fails when a team shares it. Two people apply at once, a change to a test environment accidentally touches production, or state grows so big that every plan takes ten minutes. The layout you choose and the workflow around it decide how safe and fast the team can be.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Workspaces compared with directories per environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to split state and why\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing outputs between stacks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pipeline flows: plan on review, apply on merge\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles, credentials and approvals\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-code-base-several-environments\">One code base, several environments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Workspaces\u003c/strong> give one configuration multiple state files in the same backend. They are quick for identical copies (for example, temporary preview environments), but the code cannot differ much, and it is easy to forget which workspace is selected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Directories per environment\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>envs/dev\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>envs/staging\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>envs/prod\u003c/code>) each call shared modules with their own variables and backend. They are explicit, can use different provider settings and credentials, and make a diff show exactly which environment changes. Most teams prefer this for long-lived environments.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"infra/\n  modules/\n    network/\n    app/\n  envs/\n    dev/      main.tf  backend.tf  terraform.tfvars\n    staging/  main.tf  backend.tf  terraform.tfvars\n    prod/     main.tf  backend.tf  terraform.tfvars\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">infra/\n  modules/\n    network/\n    app/\n  envs/\n    dev/      main.tf  backend.tf  terraform.tfvars\n    staging/  main.tf  backend.tf  terraform.tfvars\n    prod/     main.tf  backend.tf  terraform.tfvars\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use versioned modules (a tag or registry version) so that production uses a known version while you try a newer one in development.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"split-state-to-limit-blast-radius\">Split state to limit blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>One state for everything is slow and risky: any plan reads every resource, and a bad apply can touch everything. Split by lifecycle and ownership: networking, shared data stores, and each application in its own state. Rule of thumb: things that change together and are owned by one team belong together; things that change at different speeds belong apart.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Share values between stacks with outputs and \u003ccode>terraform_remote_state\u003c/code>, or better, by reading data sources (look up the VPC by tag) so stacks stay loosely coupled.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"team-workflow\">Team workflow\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>A change is made on a branch and a pull request opens.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The pipeline runs format, validate, lint, security checks and \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code>, and posts the plan as a comment.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A reviewer reads the plan, not just the code.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>After approval and merge, the pipeline applies the saved plan for that environment, in order: dev, staging, then production with an approval.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Only the pipeline&#39;s role may apply to production; people have read-only access.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Use remote state with locking and encryption, one pipeline job at a time per state, and \u003ccode>-lock-timeout\u003c/code> to wait instead of failing. Apply the exact plan file that was reviewed (\u003ccode>terraform apply tfplan\u003c/code>) so what was approved is what runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"credentials\">Credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prefer short-lived credentials from the pipeline&#39;s identity (OpenID Connect to the cloud provider) over stored keys. Use a separate role per environment with the least privilege needed. Keep secrets out of variables files and state outputs where possible, and mark sensitive values.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two engineers run terraform apply for the same state at the same moment. What protects the state?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> State locking in the remote backend: the second run waits or fails until the first releases the lock.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a backend that supports locking, and avoid force-unlock unless you are sure the lock is stale.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-shared-state-that-took-four-hours\">Incident: the shared state that took four hours\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An urgent security group change needs four hours because plans on the single shared state take 25 minutes and conflict with other people&#39;s applies.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"time terraform plan\nterraform state list | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">time\u003c/span> terraform plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">real    24m12s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state list | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3180\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>All environments&#39; resources lived in one state. The team split networking, data stores and each service into separate states with their own pipelines. Plans fell to about a minute, applies no longer collided, and a change to one service could not alter the network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using workspaces for very different environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One giant state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying a different plan than the reviewed one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long-lived keys in the pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"one-code-base-several-environments","text":"One code base, several environments"},{"id":"split-state-to-limit-blast-radius","text":"Split state to limit blast radius"},{"id":"team-workflow","text":"Team workflow"},{"id":"credentials","text":"Credentials"},{"id":"incident-the-shared-state-that-took-four-hours","text":"Incident: the shared state that took four hours"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform docs: Workspaces","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state/workspaces"},{"t":"Terraform docs: Remote state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state/remote"}],"text":"A Terraform setup that works for one person fails when a team shares it. Two people apply at once, a change to a test environment accidentally touches production, or state grows so big that every plan takes ten minutes. The layout you choose and the workflow around it decide how safe and fast the team can be. What you will learn Workspaces compared with directories per environment How to split state and why Sharing outputs between stacks Pipeline flows: plan on review, apply on merge Roles, credentials and approvals One code base, several environments Workspaces give one configuration multiple state files in the same backend. They are quick for identical copies (for example, temporary preview environments), but the code cannot differ much, and it is easy to forget which workspace is selected. Directories per environment ( envs/dev , envs/staging , envs/prod ) each call shared modules with their own variables and backend. They are explicit, can use different provider settings and credentials, and make a diff show exactly which environment changes. Most teams prefer this for long-lived environments. infra/ modules/ network/ app/ envs/ dev/ main.tf backend.tf terraform.tfvars staging/ main.tf backend.tf terraform.tfvars prod/ main.tf backend.tf terraform.tfvars Use versioned modules (a tag or registry version) so that production uses a known version while you try a newer one in development. Split state to limit blast radius One state for everything is slow and risky: any plan reads every resource, and a bad apply can touch everything. Split by lifecycle and ownership: networking, shared data stores, and each application in its own state. Rule of thumb: things that change together and are owned by one team belong together; things that change at different speeds belong apart. Share values between stacks with outputs and terraform_remote_state , or better, by reading data sources (look up the VPC by tag) so stacks stay loosely coupled. Team workflow A change is made on a branch and a pull request opens. The pipeline runs format, validate, lint, security checks and terraform plan , and posts the plan as a comment. A reviewer reads the plan, not just the code. After approval and merge, the pipeline applies the saved plan for that environment, in order: dev, staging, then production with an approval. Only the pipeline&#39;s role may apply to production; people have read-only access. Use remote state with locking and encryption, one pipeline job at a time per state, and -lock-timeout to wait instead of failing. Apply the exact plan file that was reviewed ( terraform apply tfplan ) so what was approved is what runs. Credentials Prefer short-lived credentials from the pipeline&#39;s identity (OpenID Connect to the cloud provider) over stored keys. Use a separate role per environment with the least privilege needed. Keep secrets out of variables files and state outputs where possible, and mark sensitive values. Predict first. Two engineers run terraform apply for the same state at the same moment. What protects the state? Your guess Answer: State locking in the remote backend: the second run waits or fails until the first releases the lock. Use a backend that supports locking, and avoid force-unlock unless you are sure the lock is stale. Incident: the shared state that took four hours Incident An urgent security group change needs four hours because plans on the single shared state take 25 minutes and conflict with other people&#39;s applies. $ time terraform plan ... real 24m12s $ terraform state list | wc -l 3180 All environments&#39; resources lived in one state. The team split networking, data stores and each service into separate states with their own pipelines. Plans fell to about a minute, applies no longer collided, and a change to one service could not alter the network. Common mistakes Using workspaces for very different environments One giant state Applying a different plan than the reviewed one Long-lived keys in the pipeline"},"terraform/import-refactor-and-moved-blocks":{"html":"\u003cp>Half of your infrastructure was created by hand in the console, and the other half sits in Terraform code that you now want to reorganize into modules. Both jobs have the same danger: Terraform decides what to destroy by comparing addresses in state with addresses in code. If those do not line up, the plan wants to delete real things.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Import existing resources with the import block\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rename and move resources with moved blocks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove a resource from management without destroying it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Always read the plan: zero destroys is the goal\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"import-existing-resources\">Import existing resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003ccode>import\u003c/code> block tells Terraform that a resource in the cloud already exists and should be tracked at a given address. It is code, so it can be reviewed, unlike an ad hoc command.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import {\n  to = aws_s3_bucket.assets\n  id = &quot;acme-assets-prod&quot;\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;assets&quot; {\n  bucket = &quot;acme-assets-prod&quot;\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  to\u003c/span> = aws_s3_bucket.assets\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  id\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-assets-prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;assets&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  bucket\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;acme-assets-prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> and the plan shows the import. Then add attributes to the resource until the plan shows no changes for it, because Terraform compares your code to the real settings. \u003ccode>terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf\u003c/code> can write a first draft of the resource for you to clean up. Imports update state only; they never change the real resource until you apply a change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rename-and-move-with-moved-blocks\">Rename and move with moved blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you rename \u003ccode>aws_instance.web\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>aws_instance.app\u003c/code>, Terraform sees one resource to destroy and a new one to create. A \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> block says it is the same object.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"moved {\n  from = aws_instance.web\n  to   = aws_instance.app\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">moved\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  from\u003c/span> = aws_instance.web\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  to\u003c/span>   = aws_instance.app\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The same works when you move a resource into a module: \u003ccode>from = aws_instance.app\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>to = module.compute.aws_instance.app\u003c/code>. The plan then reports &quot;has moved&quot; and no destroy. Keep moved blocks in the code until every environment has applied them, because each state needs to see the move once. Compared with \u003ccode>terraform state mv\u003c/code>, a moved block is reviewed, repeatable, and works the same for every environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stop-managing-without-destroying\">Stop managing without destroying\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To hand a resource to another team or tool, remove it from state without touching the real object. The \u003ccode>removed\u003c/code> block does this in code:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"removed {\n  from = aws_s3_bucket.legacy\n  lifecycle {\n    destroy = false\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">removed {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  from\u003c/span> = aws_s3_bucket.legacy\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lifecycle\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    destroy\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Without \u003ccode>destroy = false\u003c/code>, deleting the resource block would plan a destroy. Protect critical resources with \u003ccode>prevent_destroy = true\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>lifecycle\u003c/code>, so that an accidental removal fails at plan time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prove-the-refactor\">Prove the refactor\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The proof is the plan. Before and after a refactor, run \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> and read the summary line. A pure refactor should say \u003ccode>0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy\u003c/code>, with only &quot;moved&quot; or &quot;import&quot; notes. Do it first on a non-production environment with the same code, then in production through the normal pipeline, with the plan attached to the review. If anything is to be destroyed that you did not intend, stop and fix the addressing before applying.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You move a resource into a module and the plan shows 1 to destroy and 1 to add. What do you add?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A moved block from the old address to the new address inside the module.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The plan sees different addresses; moved tells Terraform they are the same object.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-plan-that-wanted-to-delete-production\">Incident: The plan that wanted to delete production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cleanup moved all network resources into a module, and the pipeline plan showed 38 resources to destroy, including the production VPC.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan | tail -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 38 to add, 0 to change, 38 to destroy.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_vpc.main will be destroyed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # module.network.aws_vpc.main will be created\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The engineer had tested only in a scratch account that had no state. Reviewers noticed the destroy lines, the change was stopped, and moved blocks were added for each resource. The plan then read &quot;38 moved&quot; with no destroys. The team added a pipeline rule that fails when a plan destroys resources tagged \u003ccode>tier=production\u003c/code>, unless a reviewer adds an explicit approval label.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Renaming resources without a moved block\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing the moved block before every environment has applied it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Importing a resource and applying before the plan shows no changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting prevent_destroy on databases and networks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"import-existing-resources","text":"Import existing resources"},{"id":"rename-and-move-with-moved-blocks","text":"Rename and move with moved blocks"},{"id":"stop-managing-without-destroying","text":"Stop managing without destroying"},{"id":"prove-the-refactor","text":"Prove the refactor"},{"id":"incident-the-plan-that-wanted-to-delete-production","text":"Incident: The plan that wanted to delete production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Import resources","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/import"},{"t":"Refactoring with moved blocks","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/refactoring"}],"text":"Half of your infrastructure was created by hand in the console, and the other half sits in Terraform code that you now want to reorganize into modules. Both jobs have the same danger: Terraform decides what to destroy by comparing addresses in state with addresses in code. If those do not line up, the plan wants to delete real things. What you will learn Import existing resources with the import block Rename and move resources with moved blocks Remove a resource from management without destroying it Always read the plan: zero destroys is the goal Import existing resources An import block tells Terraform that a resource in the cloud already exists and should be tracked at a given address. It is code, so it can be reviewed, unlike an ad hoc command. import { to = aws_s3_bucket.assets id = \"acme-assets-prod\" } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"assets\" { bucket = \"acme-assets-prod\" } Run terraform plan and the plan shows the import. Then add attributes to the resource until the plan shows no changes for it, because Terraform compares your code to the real settings. terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf can write a first draft of the resource for you to clean up. Imports update state only; they never change the real resource until you apply a change. Rename and move with moved blocks If you rename aws_instance.web to aws_instance.app , Terraform sees one resource to destroy and a new one to create. A moved block says it is the same object. moved { from = aws_instance.web to = aws_instance.app } The same works when you move a resource into a module: from = aws_instance.app and to = module.compute.aws_instance.app . The plan then reports \"has moved\" and no destroy. Keep moved blocks in the code until every environment has applied them, because each state needs to see the move once. Compared with terraform state mv , a moved block is reviewed, repeatable, and works the same for every environment. Stop managing without destroying To hand a resource to another team or tool, remove it from state without touching the real object. The removed block does this in code: removed { from = aws_s3_bucket.legacy lifecycle { destroy = false } } Without destroy = false , deleting the resource block would plan a destroy. Protect critical resources with prevent_destroy = true in lifecycle , so that an accidental removal fails at plan time. Prove the refactor The proof is the plan. Before and after a refactor, run terraform plan and read the summary line. A pure refactor should say 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy , with only \"moved\" or \"import\" notes. Do it first on a non-production environment with the same code, then in production through the normal pipeline, with the plan attached to the review. If anything is to be destroyed that you did not intend, stop and fix the addressing before applying. Predict first. You move a resource into a module and the plan shows 1 to destroy and 1 to add. What do you add? Your guess Answer: A moved block from the old address to the new address inside the module. The plan sees different addresses; moved tells Terraform they are the same object. Incident: The plan that wanted to delete production Incident A cleanup moved all network resources into a module, and the pipeline plan showed 38 resources to destroy, including the production VPC. $ terraform plan | tail -4 Plan: 38 to add, 0 to change, 38 to destroy. # aws_vpc.main will be destroyed # module.network.aws_vpc.main will be created The engineer had tested only in a scratch account that had no state. Reviewers noticed the destroy lines, the change was stopped, and moved blocks were added for each resource. The plan then read \"38 moved\" with no destroys. The team added a pipeline rule that fails when a plan destroys resources tagged tier=production , unless a reviewer adds an explicit approval label. Common mistakes Renaming resources without a moved block Removing the moved block before every environment has applied it Importing a resource and applying befo"},"terraform/large-configurations-and-performance":{"html":"\u003cp>One Terraform project grew to 1,800 resources. A plan takes eleven minutes, two engineers block each other on the state lock, and a small change to a tag shows a plan that touches the network. Size is the cause of all three. The cure is splitting by risk and by owner, not by file name.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why one giant state is slow and risky\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to split into stacks by lifecycle and ownership\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing values between stacks safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making plans faster: targeted refresh, parallelism and provider settings\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cost-of-one-big-state\">The cost of one big state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Terraform reads every resource in the state during a plan to see if it changed, and each read is an API call. More resources means a longer plan and more rate limit pressure. The state lock allows one writer at a time, so a big shared state serializes the whole team. And the \u003cstrong>blast radius\u003c/strong> grows: a mistake in a shared file can affect networking, databases and applications together.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"split-by-lifecycle-and-owner\">Split by lifecycle and owner\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Put things that change at different speeds, or belong to different teams, in separate stacks, each with its own state:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"stacks/\n  foundation/    accounts, DNS zones, identity        (changes rarely)\n  network/       VPCs, subnets, routing               (platform team)\n  data/          databases, queues, buckets           (data team)\n  apps/shop/     services, load balancers, autoscaling (shop team)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">stacks/\n  foundation/    accounts, DNS zones, identity        (changes rarely)\n  network/       VPCs, subnets, routing               (platform team)\n  data/          databases, queues, buckets           (data team)\n  apps/shop/     services, load balancers, autoscaling (shop team)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A good boundary is one where the lower stack changes much less often than the upper one, and where a different group would approve changes. Avoid splitting into dozens of tiny stacks with circular dependencies, because every connection between them is something you must keep consistent by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"share-values-between-stacks\">Share values between stacks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A stack exposes \u003cstrong>outputs\u003c/strong>, and others read them. The two common ways are reading the other state through a \u003ccode>terraform_remote_state\u003c/code> data source, and looking up the real resource by name or tag with a normal data source.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"data &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\n  tags = { Name = &quot;prod-main&quot; }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_security_group&quot; &quot;app&quot; {\n  name   = &quot;shop-app&quot;\n  vpc_id = data.aws_vpc.main.id\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">data\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_vpc&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  tags\u003c/span> = { Name = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod-main&quot;\u003c/span> }\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">resource\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;aws_security_group&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  name\u003c/span>   = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-app&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  vpc_id\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">data\u003c/span>.aws_vpc.main.id\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Looking up by tag avoids giving every consumer read access to another team&#39;s state, which can contain secrets. Publish only the values others need, for example subnet IDs, and name them as a stable interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"speed\">Speed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Several settings help once the structure is sound:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>terraform plan -refresh=false\u003c/code> skips reading real resources (use only when you know nothing changed outside Terraform).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>-target\u003c/code> limits a plan to chosen resources, but it is for emergencies, because it can leave things out of step.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>-parallelism=20\u003c/code> raises the number of concurrent operations (the default is 10), if the provider API allows it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache providers with \u003ccode>TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR\u003c/code> in the pipeline, so each run does not download them again.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Measure first. A plan that spends most of its time on one slow data source needs a fix there, not more parallelism.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two engineers wait on the state lock while each only changes their own application. What structural change helps most?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Give each application its own stack and state, so unrelated changes do not share a lock.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Locks are per state. Smaller states mean less waiting and a smaller blast radius.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-eleven-minutes-for-a-tag-change\">Incident: Eleven minutes for a tag change\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A one-line tag change needed an eleven-minute plan, and during that time nobody else could change anything in the account.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"time terraform plan\nterraform state list | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">time\u003c/span> terraform plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 0 to add, 14 to change, 0 to destroy.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">real    10m52s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform state list | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1812\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Everything lived in a single state. The team split foundation, network, data and each application into stacks. Plans fell to under a minute, the shop team no longer blocked the data team, and an application stack could not even see the network resources it did not own. They added a short README to each stack describing its outputs as its public interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Splitting by file name instead of by lifecycle and owner\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating many tiny stacks with circular dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving every stack read access to every state, including secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using -target as a normal habit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-cost-of-one-big-state","text":"The cost of one big state"},{"id":"split-by-lifecycle-and-owner","text":"Split by lifecycle and owner"},{"id":"share-values-between-stacks","text":"Share values between stacks"},{"id":"speed","text":"Speed"},{"id":"incident-eleven-minutes-for-a-tag-change","text":"Incident: Eleven minutes for a tag change"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state"},{"t":"Terraform CLI: plan options","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/plan"}],"text":"One Terraform project grew to 1,800 resources. A plan takes eleven minutes, two engineers block each other on the state lock, and a small change to a tag shows a plan that touches the network. Size is the cause of all three. The cure is splitting by risk and by owner, not by file name. What you will learn Why one giant state is slow and risky How to split into stacks by lifecycle and ownership Sharing values between stacks safely Making plans faster: targeted refresh, parallelism and provider settings The cost of one big state Terraform reads every resource in the state during a plan to see if it changed, and each read is an API call. More resources means a longer plan and more rate limit pressure. The state lock allows one writer at a time, so a big shared state serializes the whole team. And the blast radius grows: a mistake in a shared file can affect networking, databases and applications together. Split by lifecycle and owner Put things that change at different speeds, or belong to different teams, in separate stacks, each with its own state: stacks/ foundation/ accounts, DNS zones, identity (changes rarely) network/ VPCs, subnets, routing (platform team) data/ databases, queues, buckets (data team) apps/shop/ services, load balancers, autoscaling (shop team) A good boundary is one where the lower stack changes much less often than the upper one, and where a different group would approve changes. Avoid splitting into dozens of tiny stacks with circular dependencies, because every connection between them is something you must keep consistent by hand. Share values between stacks A stack exposes outputs , and others read them. The two common ways are reading the other state through a terraform_remote_state data source, and looking up the real resource by name or tag with a normal data source. data \"aws_vpc\" \"main\" { tags = { Name = \"prod-main\" } } resource \"aws_security_group\" \"app\" { name = \"shop-app\" vpc_id = data .aws_vpc.main.id } Looking up by tag avoids giving every consumer read access to another team&#39;s state, which can contain secrets. Publish only the values others need, for example subnet IDs, and name them as a stable interface. Speed Several settings help once the structure is sound: terraform plan -refresh=false skips reading real resources (use only when you know nothing changed outside Terraform). -target limits a plan to chosen resources, but it is for emergencies, because it can leave things out of step. -parallelism=20 raises the number of concurrent operations (the default is 10), if the provider API allows it. Cache providers with TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR in the pipeline, so each run does not download them again. Measure first. A plan that spends most of its time on one slow data source needs a fix there, not more parallelism. Predict first. Two engineers wait on the state lock while each only changes their own application. What structural change helps most? Your guess Answer: Give each application its own stack and state, so unrelated changes do not share a lock. Locks are per state. Smaller states mean less waiting and a smaller blast radius. Incident: Eleven minutes for a tag change Incident A one-line tag change needed an eleven-minute plan, and during that time nobody else could change anything in the account. $ time terraform plan ... Plan: 0 to add, 14 to change, 0 to destroy. real 10m52s $ terraform state list | wc -l 1812 Everything lived in a single state. The team split foundation, network, data and each application into stacks. Plans fell to under a minute, the shop team no longer blocked the data team, and an application stack could not even see the network resources it did not own. They added a short README to each stack describing its outputs as its public interface. Common mistakes Splitting by file name instead of by lifecycle and owner Creating many tiny stacks with circular dependencies Giving every stack read access to every state, including secrets Using -target as a normal habit"},"terraform/debugging-terraform":{"html":"\u003cp>Terraform failures fall into a few families: the configuration is invalid, the state does not match reality, the provider call was rejected by the cloud, or a plan wants to destroy something you did not expect. Knowing the family tells you where to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading validate, plan and apply errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turning on detailed logs with \u003ccode>TF_LOG\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>State problems: locks, drift, missing or duplicated resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why a plan shows replacement, and how to prevent it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recovering with \u003ccode>import\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>state mv\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>state rm\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-families-of-failure\">Four families of failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Family\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical message\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>First step\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Configuration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Unsupported argument, invalid reference\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform validate\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>terraform fmt -check\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>State\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Error acquiring the state lock, resource already exists\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>terraform state list\u003c/code>, check the backend\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Provider or cloud\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>403 AccessDenied, quota exceeded, name taken\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Read the API error, check permissions and limits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Plan surprise\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>must be replaced\u003c/code>, unexpected destroy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Read \u003ccode># forces replacement\u003c/code> markers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"see-what-the-provider-sent\">See what the provider sent\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"TF_LOG=DEBUG TF_LOG_PATH=./tf.log terraform plan   # may contain sensitive data, delete after use\nterraform plan -out=tfplan &amp;&amp; terraform show tfplan\nterraform plan -refresh-only                         # show drift without proposing changes\nterraform state list\nterraform state show aws_instance.web\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">TF_LOG=DEBUG TF_LOG_PATH=./tf.log terraform plan   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># may contain sensitive data, delete after use\u003c/span>\nterraform plan -out=tfplan &amp;&amp; terraform show tfplan\nterraform plan -refresh-only                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># show drift without proposing changes\u003c/span>\nterraform state list\nterraform state show aws_instance.web\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>TF_LOG=DEBUG\u003c/code> shows each API request and response, which turns &quot;Error: 403&quot; into a named permission. Delete the log file afterwards because it can contain secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-surprises\">Plan surprises\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_db_instance.main must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-/+ resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      ~ identifier = &quot;shop-db&quot; -&gt; &quot;shop-database&quot; # forces replacement\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A changed name or other immutable attribute forces replacement. For stateful resources this means data loss. Revert the change, or add \u003ccode>lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }\u003c/code> to stop an accidental destroy. When you rename a resource in code, use a \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> block or \u003ccode>terraform state mv\u003c/code> so Terraform treats it as the same object.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"repair-the-state-carefully\">Repair the state, carefully\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform import aws_s3_bucket.logs shop-logs-prod     # adopt an existing resource\nterraform state mv aws_instance.web aws_instance.app   # rename in state\nterraform state rm aws_instance.old                    # forget it without destroying it\nterraform force-unlock LOCK_ID                         # only when sure nobody is applying\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">terraform import aws_s3_bucket.logs shop-logs-prod     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># adopt an existing resource\u003c/span>\nterraform state \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mv\u003c/span> aws_instance.web aws_instance.app   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># rename in state\u003c/span>\nterraform state \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> aws_instance.old                    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># forget it without destroying it\u003c/span>\nterraform force-unlock LOCK_ID                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># only when sure nobody is applying\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Take a copy of the state first (\u003ccode>terraform state pull &gt; backup.tfstate\u003c/code>), and never edit the state file by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You rename a resource block from &quot;web&quot; to &quot;app&quot; and run plan. What does Terraform propose?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Destroy &quot;web&quot; and create &quot;app&quot;, because the address changed and no moved block tells it they are the same object.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Add a moved block (from = aws_instance.web, to = aws_instance.app) or run terraform state mv before planning.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"val&gt;plan;val&gt;state;plan&gt;api;state&gt;api;api&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Narrowing down a failed Terraform run\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"val\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Validate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read the plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"state\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">State and lock\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Provider and cloud\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix, plan again\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Validate to Read the plan; 2, Validate to State and lock; 3, Read the plan to Provider and cloud; 4, State and lock to Provider and cloud; 5, Provider and cloud to Fix, plan again.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"val\">\u003cdt>Validate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> terraform validate and fmt catch syntax and reference errors.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping it means slow cloud errors for simple typos.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Read the plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Look for replacement markers and unexpected destroys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Applying without reading the plan is how data is lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"state\">\u003cdt>State and lock \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> state list, show and the backend lock status.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A stale lock or drifted resource blocks or misleads the plan.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>Provider and cloud \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> TF_LOG shows the API call and the exact denial or quota error.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing permissions look like random failures without the log.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix, plan again \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change code or state, then confirm with a clean plan.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An apply that is not followed by a clean plan hides leftovers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-plan-that-wanted-to-delete-the-database\">Incident: the plan that wanted to delete the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A harmless-looking refactor shows \u003ccode>1 to add, 1 to destroy\u003c/code> on the production database.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan | grep -B2 -A2 &quot;forces replacement&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan | grep -B2 -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;forces replacement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # module.db.aws_db_instance.main must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      ~ db_subnet_group_name = &quot;shop-a&quot; -&gt; &quot;shop-b&quot; # forces replacement\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The refactor changed the subnet group name, which RDS cannot change in place. The reviewer caught the destroy line, so the team reverted the name, added \u003ccode>prevent_destroy\u003c/code>, and added a pipeline rule that fails when a plan destroys a tagged production resource.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Applying a plan nobody read\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>force-unlock\u003c/code> while another run is active\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hand-editing state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving \u003ccode>TF_LOG\u003c/code> files that contain secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"four-families-of-failure","text":"Four families of failure"},{"id":"see-what-the-provider-sent","text":"See what the provider sent"},{"id":"plan-surprises","text":"Plan surprises"},{"id":"repair-the-state-carefully","text":"Repair the state, carefully"},{"id":"incident-the-plan-that-wanted-to-delete-the-database","text":"Incident: the plan that wanted to delete the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform docs: Debugging","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/internals/debugging"},{"t":"Terraform docs: Moved blocks","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/refactoring"}],"text":"Terraform failures fall into a few families: the configuration is invalid, the state does not match reality, the provider call was rejected by the cloud, or a plan wants to destroy something you did not expect. Knowing the family tells you where to look. What you will learn Reading validate, plan and apply errors Turning on detailed logs with TF_LOG State problems: locks, drift, missing or duplicated resources Why a plan shows replacement, and how to prevent it Recovering with import , state mv and state rm Four families of failure Family Typical message First step Configuration Unsupported argument, invalid reference terraform validate , terraform fmt -check State Error acquiring the state lock, resource already exists terraform state list , check the backend Provider or cloud 403 AccessDenied, quota exceeded, name taken Read the API error, check permissions and limits Plan surprise must be replaced , unexpected destroy Read # forces replacement markers See what the provider sent TF_LOG=DEBUG TF_LOG_PATH=./tf.log terraform plan # may contain sensitive data, delete after use terraform plan -out=tfplan && terraform show tfplan terraform plan -refresh-only # show drift without proposing changes terraform state list terraform state show aws_instance.web TF_LOG=DEBUG shows each API request and response, which turns \"Error: 403\" into a named permission. Delete the log file afterwards because it can contain secrets. Plan surprises # aws_db_instance.main must be replaced -/+ resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" { ~ identifier = \"shop-db\" -> \"shop-database\" # forces replacement A changed name or other immutable attribute forces replacement. For stateful resources this means data loss. Revert the change, or add lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true } to stop an accidental destroy. When you rename a resource in code, use a moved block or terraform state mv so Terraform treats it as the same object. Repair the state, carefully terraform import aws_s3_bucket.logs shop-logs-prod # adopt an existing resource terraform state mv aws_instance.web aws_instance.app # rename in state terraform state rm aws_instance.old # forget it without destroying it terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID # only when sure nobody is applying Take a copy of the state first ( terraform state pull > backup.tfstate ), and never edit the state file by hand. Predict first. You rename a resource block from \"web\" to \"app\" and run plan. What does Terraform propose? Your guess Answer: Destroy \"web\" and create \"app\", because the address changed and no moved block tells it they are the same object. Add a moved block (from = aws_instance.web, to = aws_instance.app) or run terraform state mv before planning. Narrowing down a failed Terraform run Flow in order: 1, Validate to Read the plan; 2, Validate to State and lock; 3, Read the plan to Provider and cloud; 4, State and lock to Provider and cloud; 5, Provider and cloud to Fix, plan again. Step Data store Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Validate Step What it does: terraform validate and fmt catch syntax and reference errors. If it fails or is weak: Skipping it means slow cloud errors for simple typos. Read the plan Step What it does: Look for replacement markers and unexpected destroys. If it fails or is weak: Applying without reading the plan is how data is lost. State and lock Data store What it does: state list, show and the backend lock status. If it fails or is weak: A stale lock or drifted resource blocks or misleads the plan. Provider and cloud Outside party What it does: TF_LOG shows the API call and the exact denial or quota error. If it fails or is weak: Missing permissions look like random failures without the log. Fix, plan again Step What it does: Change code or state, then confirm with a clean plan. If it fails or is weak: An apply that is not followed by a clean plan hides leftovers. Incident: the plan that wanted to delete the d"}});